[{"id":"chem-2019-q1","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":1,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Nomenclature","iq":"How do we systematically name organic chemical compounds?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-7"],"parts":[],"questionText":"1 Which structural formula represents pentan-2-one?\nO\nH C C\nH\nH H H\nH H\nC C\nH\nH\nC H\nB.\nH C H\nH\nH\nH\nH H\nH\nC C\nH\nOH H\nH\nC C\nC.\nH C\nH\nH\nH\nH H O\nH\nC C C\nH\nH\nC H\nD.\nH C O H\nH\nH HO\nH\nC C\nH\nH H\nH\nC C\nA.","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q1-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2019-q2","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":2,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Properties of Acids and Bases","iq":"What is an acid and what is a base?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-7"],"parts":[],"questionText":"2 Which of the following is an Arrhenius base?\nA. Na\nB. NaOH\nC. Na2CO3\nD. NaHCO3\n– 3 –","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q2-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2019-q3","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":3,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Solution Equilibria","iq":"How does solubility relate to chemical equilibrium?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"3 Which of the following metal carbonates has the highest molar solubility?\nA. Calcium carbonate\nB. Copper(II) carbonate\nC. Iron(II) carbonate\nD. Lead(II) carbonate","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q3-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2019-q4","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":4,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[],"questionText":"4 The diagram shows the mass spectrum of an organic compound.\n0\n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\n100\n20 40 60\nm/z\nRelative abundance\n80 100\n27\n73\n88\n41\n60\nWhich compound was analysed?\nA. Butan-1-amine\nB. Butanoic acid\nC. Ethanoic acid\nD. Iron(II) sulfide\n– 4 –\nThe diagram represents the titration curve for a reaction between a particular acid an\nparticular base. Use the diagram to answer Questions 5 and 6.\nd a\n0\n2\n4\n6\n8\n10\n12\n14\n0","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q4-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2019-q5","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":5,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Properties of Acids and Bases","iq":"What is an acid and what is a base?"},{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-4"],"parts":[],"questionText":"5 10 15 20 25\nVolume of acid added (mL)\n30 35 40 45 50\npH\n5 Which indicator would be best for this titration?\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.\nIndicator Colour change range (pH)\nMartius yellow 2.0 – 3.2\nMagdala red 3.0 – 4.0\nIsopicramic acid 4.0 – 5.6\nCresol red 7.2 – 8.8","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q5-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2019-q6","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":6,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[],"questionText":"6 Which of the following equations best represents the reaction described by the titration\ncurve?\nA. NH3(aq) + HCl(aq) NH4Cl(aq)\nB. NaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) NaCl(aq) + H2O(l)\nC. NH (aq) + CH aq aq 3 3COOH( ) CH3COONH4( )\nD. NaOH(aq) + CH3COOH(aq) CH3COONa(aq) + H2O(l)\n– 5 –","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q6-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2019-q7","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":7,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Factors that Affect Equilibrium","iq":"What factors affect equilibrium and how?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"7 How does the addition of a catalyst affect a reversible reaction?\nA. It increases the activation energy of the forward reaction only.\nB. It decreases the activation energy of the forward reaction only.\nC. It increases the activation energy of both the forward and reverse reactions.\nD. It decreases the activation energy of both the forward and reverse reactions.","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q7-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2019-q8","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":8,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[],"questionText":"8 The structure of an organic compound is shown.\nCH3CH2\nO\nO C CH2CH2CH2CH3\nWhich row of the table correctly gives the name of the compound and one of the reactants\nused to produce it in a one-step reaction?\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.\nName Reactant\nEthyl pentanoate Ethanol\nEthyl pentanoate Pentan-1-ol\nPent-1-yl ethanoate Ethanol\nPent-1-yl ethanoate Pentan-1-ol","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q8-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2019-q9","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":9,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Hydrocarbons","iq":"How can hydrocarbons be classified based on their structure and reactivity?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-4","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[],"questionText":"9 All of the following compounds have similar molar masses.\nWhich has the highest boiling point?\nA. Butane\nB. Ethanoic acid\nC. Propan-1-ol\nD. Propanone","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q9-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2019-q10","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":10,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Nomenclature","iq":"How do we systematically name organic chemical compounds?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"10 Which class of organic compound must contain at least three carbon atoms?\nA. Aldehydes\nB. Alkenes\nC. Carboxylic acids\nD. Ketones\n– 6 –","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q10-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2019-q11","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":11,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Static and Dynamic Equilibrium","iq":"What happens when chemical reactions do not go through to completion?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"11 A saturated solution of barium carbonate was stored in a flask. Solid barium carbonate\ncontaining radioactive carbon-14 was added to the solution. The mixture was allowed to\nstand for several days and was then filtered.\nRadioactivity could reasonably be expected to be found in\nA. the filtrate only.\nB. the residue only.\nC. both residue and filtrate.\nD. neither residue nor filtrate.","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q11-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2019-q12","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":12,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Factors that Affect Equilibrium","iq":"What factors affect equilibrium and how?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"12 Methanol can be produced from the reaction of carbon monoxide and hydrogen,\naccording to the following equation:\nCO(g) + 2H2(g) W CH3OH(g) Dr\nH ° = −90 kJ mol−1 ̄\nWhich set of conditions will produce the maximum yield of methanol?\nA. Low pressure and low temperature\nB. Low pressure and high temperature\nC. High pressure and low temperature\nD. High pressure and high temperature","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q12-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2019-q13","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":13,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Polymers","iq":"What are the properties and uses of polymers?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"13 A sample of polydifluoroethylene is determined to have an average molar mass of\n4.8 × 104\ng mol−1.\nApproximately how many carbon atoms are there in an average molecule?\nA. 750\nB. 1500\nC. 2500\nD. 4000\n– 7 –","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q13-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2019-q14","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":14,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"14 A molecule, C4H9Cl, is analysed. The 1\nH NMR spectrum of this molecule is shown.\n10.0 9.0 8.0 7.0 6.0 5.0\nChemical shift (ppm)\n4.0 3.0 2.0 1.0 0.0\nWhat is the structural formula of this molecule?\nCH3\nCH3\nCH3\nA. C Cl\nD. CH3 CH2 CH2 CH2 Cl\nCl\nC. CH3 CH2 CH CH3\nCH3\nB. CH3 CH CH2 Cl\n– 8 –","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q14-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2019-q15","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":15,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"15 What is the concentration of hydroxide ions (in mol L–1) in a solution that has a pH of\n8.53?\nA. 3.0 × 10–9\nB. 3.4 × 10–6\nC. 5.5\nD. 3.0 × 105","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q15-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2019-q16","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":16,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)","iq":"How can the position of equilibrium be described and what does the equilibrium constant represent?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"16 At equilibrium, a 1.00 L vessel contains 0.0430 mol of H2, 0.0620 mol of I2, and\n0.358 mol of HI. The system is represented by the following equation:\nH2(g) + I2(g) W 2HI(g)\nWhich of the following is closest to the value of the equilibrium constant, K\neq, for this\nreaction?\nA. 0.0208\nB. 48.1\nC. 134\nD. 269","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q16-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2019-q17","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":17,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)","iq":"How can the position of equilibrium be described and what does the equilibrium constant represent?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-4","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[],"questionText":"17 A student makes a solution with a final volume of 200 mL by mixing 100 mL of\n0.0500 mol L–1 barium nitrate solution with 100 mL of 0.100 mol L–1 sodium hydroxide\nsolution.\nWhich row of the table correctly identifies if a precipitate will form under these conditions\nand the reason?\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.\nWill a precipitate form? Reason\nYes Q > K\nsp\nYes Q < K\nsp\nNo Q > K\nsp\nNo Q < K\nsp\n– 9 –","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q17-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2019-q18","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":18,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Solution Equilibria","iq":"How does solubility relate to chemical equilibrium?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"18 Consider the following equilibrium.\nHF(aq) + CF3COO−(aq) W F −(aq) + CF3COOH(aq) K\neq = 3.80 × 10 −4\nWhich row of the table correctly identifies the strongest acid and the strongest base in\nthis system?\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.\nStrongest acid Strongest base\nCF3COOH(aq) F −(aq)\nCF3COOH(aq) CF3COO−(aq)\nHF(aq) F −(aq)\nHF(aq) CF3COO−(aq)","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q18-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2019-q19","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":19,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-4","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[],"questionText":"19 Compound X shows three signals in its 13C NMR spectrum.\nTreatment of X with hot acidified potassium permanganate produces a compound Y.\nCompound Y turns blue litmus red.\nCompound X produces compound Z upon reaction with hot concentrated sulfuric acid.\nWhich of the following correctly identifies compounds X, Y and Z?\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.\nCompound X Compound Y Compound Z\nbutan-1-ol butanoic acid but-1-ene\nbutan-2-ol butanone but-2-ene\nmethyl ethanoate methanoic acid ethene\n2-methylpropan-1-ol 2-methylpropanoic acid 2-methylprop-1-ene\n– 10 –","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q19-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2019-q20","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":20,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"20 The manganese content in a 12.0-gram sample of steel was determined by measuring the\nabsorbance of permanganate (MnO –\n4 ) using the following process.\nThe steel sample was dissolved in nitric acid and the Mn2+(aq) ions produced were\noxidised to MnO –\n4 (aq) by periodate ions, IO –\n(aq 4 ), according to the following equation.\n2Mn2+(aq) + 5IO –\n4 (aq) + 3H O(l) 2MnO –\n(aq 2 4 ) + 5IO –\n3 (aq) + 6H+(aq)\nThe resulting solution was made up to a volume of 1.00 L, then 20.0 mL of this solution\nwas diluted to 100.0 mL. The absorbance at 525 nm of the resulting solution was 0.50.\nA calibration curve for MnO –\n4 (aq) was constructed and is shown below.\n5 15 25 10\n0.20\n0\n0.40\n0.60\n0.80\n1.00\n1.20\n20 30 35 45 55\nConcentration of MnO4\n–\n(aq) (mg L–1)\nAbsorbance\n40 50\nWhat was the percentage by mass of manganese in the steel sample?\nA. 0.019%\nB. 0.096%\nC. 0.48%\nD. 1.0%\nBLANK PAGE\n– 11 –\nBLANK PAGE\n© 2019 NSW Education Standards Authority\n– 12 –\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 13 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2019 HIGHER SCHOOL CERTIFICATE EXAMINATION\nChemistry\nSection II Answer Booklet\n80 marks\nAttempt Questions 21–34\nAllow about 2 hours and 25 minutes for this section\nInstructions • Write your Centre Number and Student Number at the top of this\npage.\n• Answer the questions in the spaces provided. These spaces\nprovide guidance for the expected length of response.\n• Show all relevant working in questions involving calculations.\n• Extra writing space is provided at the back of this booklet.\nIf you use this space, clearly indicate which question you are\nanswering.\nPlease turn over\nCentre Number\nStudent Number\n5638310365\n1011 15050\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 14 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0589310365","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q20-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2019-q21","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":21,"section":"II","marks":7,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Nomenclature","iq":"How do we systematically name organic chemical compounds?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-2","12-3","12-5","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":2,"content":["Mod 7 Nomenclature","Mod 7 Alcohols"]},{"part":"b","marks":2,"content":["Mod 7 Nomenclature"]},{"part":"c","marks":3,"content":["Mod 7 Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","Mod 7 Alcohols"]}],"questionText":"Question 21 (7 marks)\n(a) The structural formula for 2-methylpropan-2-ol is shown in the table.\nDraw one structural isomer of this alcohol and state its name.\nH\nH\nC\nOH\nC\nH\nC H\nH H C H H\nH\nStructure\nAlcohol Isomer\nName 2-methylpropan-2-ol\n2\n(b) The structural formulae for two compounds are shown below.\nC\nH\nH O H\nH\nH C C H\nIsomer A\nO\nC H\nH\nH\nH\nH\nH C C\nIsomer B\nWhy are these two compounds classed as functional group isomers?\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\n(c) A chemical test is required to distinguish between the isomers in part (b).\nIdentify a suitable test and explain the expected observations.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n3\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 15 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n1024310360","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q21-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q21-mg0.webp","/img/c2019-q21-mg1.webp","/img/c2019-q21-mg3.webp"],"mgText":"Question 21 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Provides an acceptable structure and name 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nH H H H\nH C C C C OH\nH H H H\nbutan-1-ol\nAnswers could include:\nCondensed or skeletal formulae. Other unambiguous systematic names are acceptable, eg\n1-butanol.\nQuestion 21 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• States why the two compounds are classed as functional group isomers 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nBoth compounds have the same molecular formula (C3H6O) but have different functional\ngroups.\nQuestion 21 (c)\nCriteria Marks\n• Identifies a suitable test\n• Provides the appropriate observations 3\n• Explains the observations\n• Identifies the observations and the test reagent\nOR 2\n• Explains the observations\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nTollens' test could be used to distinguish between Isomer A (ketone) and Isomer B\n(aldehyde). Aldehydes are readily oxidised to carboxylic acids whereas ketones are not.\nIsomer B will therefore reduce the silver ions in the Tollens’ reagent to form a silver mirror\ninside the test tube whereas Isomer A will not react.\nAnswers could include:\nOther oxidants that can oxidise Isomer B but not Isomer A, eg Cr2O 2−\n7 , MnO −4 .\nI /OH−\n2 will give iodoform with Isomer A but not with Isomer B.\nThe orange dichromate solution will change to green.\nThe purple permanganate solution will decolourise.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q22","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":22,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-4","12-5"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 6 Quantitative Analysis"]}],"questionText":"Question 22 (4 marks)\nA buffer was prepared with acetic acid and sodium acetate. A few drops of universal\nindicator were then added. When small amounts of either 0.1 mol L–1 HCl(aq) or\n0.1 mol L–1 NaOH(aq) were added, no change in the colour of the solution was\nobserved.\nExplain these observations. Support your answer with at least ONE chemical equation.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nPlease turn over\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 16 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n5486310366","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q22-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q22-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 22\nCriteria Marks\n• Identifies that the observed effect is due to small variation in pH\n• Explains what occurs when acid and base are added\n• Includes at least one equation\n4\n• Identifies that the observed effect is due to small variation in pH\n• Explains what occurs when acid or base is added\n• Includes a substantially correct equation\n3\n• Provides a correct equation\n• OR\n• Includes a partially correct equation and shows some understanding of\nwhat occurs when acid or base is added\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nThe colour of the universal indicator did not change because the pH did not change very\nmuch despite the addition of acid and/or base. This is due to the equilibrium position of the\nbuffer equation shifting in response to the addition of acid and/or base in accordance with\nLe Chatelier’s Principle.\nCH3COOH(aq) + H2O(l) \u0001 CH3COO–\n(aq) + H3O+(aq)\nIf acid is added the equilibrium position will shift left and if base is added the equilibrium\nposition shifts right. In both cases the concentration of H3O+ remains nearly constant.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q23","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":23,"section":"II","marks":6,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"}],"outcomes":["12-1","12-14","12-2","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":4,"content":["Mod 7 Alcohols"]},{"part":"b","marks":2,"content":["Mod 7 Alcohols"]}],"questionText":"Question 23 (6 marks)\nThe following apparatus was used in an experiment to determine the molar enthalpy\nof combustion of ethanol.\nThermometer\nAluminium beaker\n105 g water\nGauze\nTripod\nSpirit burner\ncontaining ethanol\n(a) Calculate the experimental molar enthalpy of combustion (DcH) of ethanol\nwhen 0.370 g ethanol was used to raise the water temperature from 18.5°C to\n30.0°C.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n4\nQuestion 23 continues on page 17\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 17 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n7986310363\nQuestion 23 (continued)\n(b) Upon replication, the molar enthalpy of combustion obtained in the experiment\nwas consistently much lower than the accepted value.\nExplain ONE change that could be made to the experiment that would improve\nthe accuracy of the obtained value.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\nEnd of Question 23\nPlease turn over\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 18 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0736310369","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q23-0.webp","/img/c2019-q23-1.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q23-mg0.webp","/img/c2019-q23-mg1.webp"],"mgText":"Question 23 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nCorrectly calculates \u0001 H with units c\nUses correct significant figures\n4\n• Provides substantially correct working 3\n• Provides some relevant steps 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nMoles ethanol (n) = 0.370 g / (12.01 × 2 + 1.008 × 6 + 16.00 g mol–1)\n= 0.008032 mol\nq = mC\u0001T\n= 105 g × 4.18 J g−1K−1 × (30 – 18.5)K\n= 5047.35 J\nq 5047.35 J \u0001 H = − = − c n 0.008032 mol\n= −628405 Jmol−1\n= −628 kJmol−1 (3 significant figures)\nQuestion 23 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• Provides a change that would improve accuracy\n• Gives a reason for the low molar enthalpy of combustion related to the\nchange\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nThe experimental value for the molar enthalpy of combustion is low, primarily due to heat\nloss to the environment.\nA change that would reduce heat loss and improve accuracy is moving the spirit burner\ncloser to the beaker.\n(There are many other possible improvements that are acceptable.)","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q24","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":24,"section":"II","marks":7,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-4","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":3,"content":["Mod 6 Quantitative Analysis"]},{"part":"b","marks":4,"content":["Mod 6 Quantitative Analysis"]}],"questionText":"Question 24 (7 marks)\nA conductometric titration was undertaken to determine the concentration of a\nbarium hydroxide solution. The solution was added to 250.0 mL of standardised\n1.050 × 10–3 mol L–1 hydrochloric acid solution. The results of the titration are shown\nin the conductivity graph.\n0 5 10 15\nVolume of barium hydroxide (mL)\n20 25 30\nConductivity\n(a) Explain the shape of the titration curve.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n3\n(b) The equivalence point was reached when a volume of 17.15 mL of barium\nhydroxide was added.\nCalculate the concentration of barium hydroxide (in mol L–1), and give a relevant\nchemical equation.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 19 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n6277310365","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q24-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q24-mg0.webp","/img/c2019-q24-mg1.webp"],"mgText":"Question 24 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Explains the shape of the titration curve, in terms of ions present, as it\nfalls, at equivalence point and as it rises 3\n• Describes the trend in the titration curve and makes an explanation 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nThe curve steadily falls as OH–\nions are added because they react and remove the highly\nconductive H+ ions from the solution. At equivalence point, conductivity is lowest because\nthe solution contains only Ba2+ and Cl–\nions, which are much less mobile than the H+ or OH–\nions. The curve then rises as more OH–\nions are added.\nQuestion 24 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• Provides correct calculation, and a balanced equation with states 4\n•\nOR\nProvides partially correct calculation and a correct balanced equation\n3\n• Provides substantially correct calculation\n• Provides some relevant steps 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\n2HCl(aq) + Ba(OH)2(aq) → BaCl2(aq) + 2H2O(l)\nn HCl) = c × v = 1.050 × 10–3 molL–1 ( × 250.0\n1\nn(Ba(OH)2 ) = n(HCl) ×\n2\t (as reaction ratio is 2 :1)\nn 1.050 × 10–3 × 0.2500 L Concentration Ba(OH)2 = = v 0.01715 × 2\n= 7.653 × 10–3 molL–1","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q25","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":25,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Factors that Affect Equilibrium","iq":"What factors affect equilibrium and how?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-4","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":2,"content":["Mod 5 Factors that Affect Equilibrium"]},{"part":"b","marks":3,"content":["Mod 5 Factors that Affect Equilibrium"]}],"questionText":"Question 25 (5 marks)\nThe concentrations of reactants and products as a function of time for the followin\nsystem were determined.\nCO(g) + H O(g 2 ) W CO2(g) + H (g 2 )\nAt time T, some CO(g) was removed from the system.\n(a) The concentration of CO after time T is shown.\nSketch the concentrations after time T for the remaining species.\ng\nTime (min)\nConcentration (mol L–1)\nH2\nH2O\nCO2\nCO\nT\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\n(b) Using collision theory, explain the change in the concentration of CO after\ntime T.\n3\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 20 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n1032310369","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q25-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q25-mg0.webp","/img/c2019-q25-mg1.webp"],"mgText":"Question 25 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Sketches the concentrations correctly 2\n• Provides some correct features 1\nSample answer:\nQuestion 25 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• Provides a thorough explanation of the change in the concentration of CO 3\n• Provides some explanation of the change in the concentration of CO 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nAt time T, the concentration of CO was decreased.\nA decrease in the concentration of CO results in a decrease in the rate of the forward\nreaction due to fewer collisions between CO and H2O molecules.\nThe rate of the reverse reaction is now greater than the forward reaction so the\nconcentrations of H2(g) and CO2(g) decrease. As this occurs, the concentrations of CO(g)\nand H2O(g) gradually increase.\nThe rate of the forward reaction subsequently increases until at some point the rate of the\nreverse reaction will be the same as the rate of the forward reaction as equilibrium is\nestablished.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q26","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":26,"section":"II","marks":8,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-2","12-4","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":5,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Organic Substances"]},{"part":"b","marks":3,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Organic Substances"]}],"questionText":"Question 26 (8 marks)\nThe following data were obtained for an organic compound containing carbon,\nhydrogen and oxygen. The compound is a colourless liquid that reacts with sodium\ncarbonate powder to produce bubbles.\n0\n10\n20\n30\n50\n70\n100\n90\n40\n60\n80\n20\n45\n57\n73\n74\n40 60 80 100\nm/z\nRelative abundance\nMass spectrum\nTransmittance\nInfrared spectrum\n0.2\n3000 2000\nWavenumber (cm–1)\n1000\n0.4\n0.6\n0.8\n1\n500\n1.00 2.00 3.00\n13C NMR spectrum\n200 180 160 140 120 100 80 60 40 20 0\nChemical shift (ppm)\n1\nH NMR spectrum\nChemical shift (ppm)\n12 10 8 6 4 2 0\nQuestion 26 continues on page 21\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 21 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0136310363\nQuestion 26 (continued)\n(a) What is the structural formula of this compound? Justify your answer with\nreference to the information given on its reactivity and to at least THREE of the\nprovided spectra.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n5\n(b) Explain why a chemist should use more than one spectroscopic technique to\nidentify an organic compound. Use TWO spectroscopic techniques to support\nyour answer.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n3\nEnd of Question 26\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 22 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n7037310360","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q26-0.webp","/img/c2019-q26-1.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q26-mg0.webp","/img/c2019-q26-mg2.webp"],"mgText":"Question 26 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Gives correct structure and justifies using reactivity and at least three\nspectra 5\n•\nOR\nGives substantially correct structure and justifies using reactivity and at\nleast two spectra\n4\n• Gives a correct structure and justifies using at least three spectra\n• Gives substantially correct structure and some correct analyses\nOR 2–3\n• Gives substantially correct analyses with incorrect structure\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nH H O\nH C C C\nH H OH\nReaction with sodium carbonate implies that it is an organic acid as shown.\nThe 13C NMR spectrum has three peaks; the peak at 180 ppm confirms the presence of a\nCOOH group.\nThe 1\nH NMR spectrum shows 3 separate proton environments – the splitting and integration\nindicate a CH3 group and a CH2 group.\nThe mass spectrum has a parent ion at 74, indicating that the compound must have a molar\nmass of 74 g mol−1\n.\nAnswers could include:\nCondensed or skeletal formula.\nCould use infrared to confirm the presence of a carboxylic acid group.\nQuestion 26 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nExplains why more than one spectroscopic technique should be used\nSupports answer with two spectroscopic techniques\n3\n•\n•\nOutlines why more than one spectroscopic technique should be used\nSupports answer with at least one spectroscopic technique\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nDifferent techniques are used in the identification of organic molecules but give different\ninformation about structure.\nFor example, 1\nH NMR provides information about the chemical environment and relative\nnumber of hydrogen nuclei. This can be used to identify functional groups and distinguish\nbetween isomers.\nMass spectrometry gives information about the molecular weight of a molecule and its\ncharacteristic fragments.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q27","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":27,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"},{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"},{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)","iq":"How can the position of equilibrium be described and what does the equilibrium constant represent?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-13","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":1,"content":["Mod 6 Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","Mod 6 Quantitative Analysis","Mod 5 Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)"]},{"part":"b","marks":4,"content":["Mod 6 Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","Mod 6 Quantitative Analysis","Mod 5 Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)"]}],"questionText":"Question 27 (5 marks)\nThe relationship between the acid dissociation constant, Ka , and the corresponding\nconjugate base dissociation constant, Kb , is given by:\nKa b w × K = K\nAssume that the temperature for part (a) and part (b) is 25°C.\n(a) The Ka of hypochlorous acid (HOCl) is 3.0 × 10−8.\nShow that the K of the hypochlorite ion, OCl–\n, is 3.3 × 10−7. b\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n1\n(b) The conjugate base dissociation constant, Kb , is the equilibrium constant for the\nfollowing equation:\nOCl–\n(aq) + H2O(l) W HOCl(aq) + OH–(aq)\nCalculate the pH of a 0.20 mol L−1 solution of sodium hypochlorite (NaOCl).\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 23 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2207310364","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q27-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q27-mg0.webp","/img/c2019-q27-mg2.webp"],"mgText":"Question 27 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Provides correct method for calculating Kb 1\nSample answer:\nK 1.0 × 10–14\nw K = = = 3.3 × 10–7\nb K 3.0 × 10–8\na\nQuestion 27 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly calculates pH, showing all working 4\n• Provides substantially correct working 3\n• Provides some relevant steps 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\n⎡ ⎤ ⎡ ⎤ HOCI ⎦⎥ OH–\n⎣⎢ ⎣⎢ ⎦⎥ = 3.3 × 10–7\n⎡ ⎤ OCI–\n⎣⎢ ⎦⎥\nBecause Kb is very small we can assume that the equilibrium concentration of OCI– is\nessentially unchanged.\nH2\nO + OCl– \u0001 HOCl + OH–\nI\nC\nE\n0.20\n–x\n≈ 0.20\n0\n+x\nx\n0\n+x\nx\nNow, ⎡ ⎤ ⎡ = – ⎤ HOCl at equilibrium ⎣⎢ ⎥⎦ ⎣⎢OH ⎥⎦\nx2\n∴ = 3.3 × 10–7\n0.20\nx = (3.3 × 10–7 ) × 0.20\n= 2.5690 × 10–4 mol L–1\n∴ ⎡ ⎤ OH– = 2.5690 × –4 –1 10 mol L ⎣⎢ ⎥⎦\npOH = −log (⎡ – ⎤ ⎢OH ⎣ ⎦⎥) = −log 2.5690 × 10–4 ( )\n= 3.59\npH = 14 − pOH\n= 10.41","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q28","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":28,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Properties of Acids and Bases","iq":"What is an acid and what is a base?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":5,"content":["Mod 6 Properties of Acids and Bases"]}],"questionText":"Question 28 (5 marks)\nAssess the usefulness of the Brønsted–Lowry model in classifying acids and bases.\nSupport your answer with at least TWO chemical equations.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n5\nPlease turn over\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 24 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n1619310360","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q28-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q28-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 28\nCriteria Marks\n• Makes an informed judgement about the usefulness of the B–L model\n• Considers an advantage and a limitation of the model\n• Includes at least TWO relevant equations\n5\n• Makes a judgement about the usefulness of the B–L model\n• Considers an advantage and a limitation of the model\n• Includes at least ONE relevant equation\nOR\n• Outlines an advantage and a limitation of the B–L model\n• Includes at least TWO relevant equations\n4\n• Outlines an advantage and/or a limitation of the model\nAND/OR\n• Includes at least ONE relevant equation\n2–3\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nThe Brønsted–Lowry model describes acids as proton donors and bases as proton\nacceptors. It was an improvement on the Arrhenius theory as it describes more acid and\nbase behaviour.\nFor example, in the reaction NH3(g) + HCl(g) → NH4Cl(s), ammonia is classified as a\nBrønsted–Lowry base and hydrogen chloride as a Brønsted–Lowry acid, as a proton has\nbeen transferred from the hydrogen chloride to the ammonia. Ammonia is not an Arrhenius\nbase as it does not dissociate to form OH− ions.\nHowever, the Brønsted–Lowry model does not explain the acidity of acidic oxides such as\nSO2 and SO3 and their reaction with basic oxides such as CaO. For example, in the reaction\nCaO(s) + SO3(g) → CaSO4(s) there is no proton transfer, so Brønsted–Lowry theory cannot\nbe used to explain this as an acid–base reaction.\nThe Brønsted–Lowry model is useful to explain many acid–base reactions but has its\nlimitations.\nAnswers could include\nDescriptions of Lewis acid–base reactions.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q29","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":29,"section":"II","marks":11,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"},{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Solution Equilibria","iq":"How does solubility relate to chemical equilibrium?"}],"outcomes":["12-1","12-12","12-15","12-2","12-4","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":2,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Inorganic Substances","Mod 5 Solution Equilibria"]},{"part":"b","marks":2,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Inorganic Substances"]},{"part":"c","marks":7,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Inorganic Substances"]}],"questionText":"Question 29 (11 marks)\nStormwater from a mine site has been found to be contaminated with copper(II) and\nlead(II) ions. The required discharge limit is 1.0 mg L–1 for each metal ion. Treatment\nof the stormwater with Ca(OH)2 solid to remove the metal ions is recommended.\n(a) Explain the recommended treatment with reference to solubility. Include a\nrelevant chemical equation.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\n(b) Explain why atomic absorption spectroscopy can be used to determine the\nconcentrations of Cu2+ and Pb2+ ions in a solution containing both species.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\n(c) The data below were obtained after treatment of the stormwater. 7\nData from atomic absorption spectroscopy\nConcentration (× 10–5 mol L–1) Absorbance\nCu2+ or Pb2+ Cu2+ Pb2+\n0.0 0.000 0.000\n1.0 0.140 0.090\n2.0 0.310 0.180\n4.0 0.520 0.390\n6.0 0.840 0.530\nWater sample before treatment 0.820 0.440\nWater sample after treatment 0.040 0.080\nQuestion 29 continues on page 25\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 25 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0585310361\nQuestion 29 (continued)\nTo what extent is the treatment effective in meeting the required discharge limit\nof 1.0 mg L–1 for each metal ion? Support your conclusion with calibration\ncurves and calculations.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\nEnd of Question 29\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 26 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n6577310368","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q29-0.webp","/img/c2019-q29-1.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q29-mg0.webp","/img/c2019-q29-mg1.webp","/img/c2019-q29-mg3.webp","/img/c2019-q29-mg4.webp"],"mgText":"Question 29 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nExplains the treatment with reference to solubility\nProvides a correct chemical equation\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nCalcium hydroxide, Ca(OH)2, is slightly soluble in water. Copper(II) hydroxide and lead(II)\nhydroxide are both very insoluble in water shown by their small Ksp values, which will result\nin most of the metal ions precipitating.\nA chemical equation Cu(OH)2(s) \u0001 Cu2+(aq) + 2OH–\n(aq)\nAnswers could include:\nAlternative chemical equations:\nPb(OH)2(s) \u0001 Pb2+(aq) + 2OH–\n(aq)\nM(OH) (s) \u0001 Mn+(aq) + nOH–(aq) n\nQuestion 29 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• Provides a valid explanation 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nAAS determines the concentration of metal ions by measuring the absorbance of light at\ndifferent characteristic wavelengths for each metal. This allows the determination of the\nconcentration of one metal without interference from the other metal.\nQuestion 29 (c)\nCriteria Marks\n• Provides correct graph\n– Labels axes correctly, including units\n– Uses appropriate scale\n– Plots both sets of data\n•\n– Draws two lines of best fit\n– Provides a key\nProvides correct concentration values\n7\n•\n•\nConverts concentration data from mol L–1 to mg L–1\nCompares to discharge limit and makes correct judgement of treatment\nsuccess\n• Provides a substantially correct answer with a minor error 6\n• Provides correct graph, provides correct concentration values and applies\nconversion\nOR 5\n• Provides a substantially correct graph, provides concentration values,\napplies conversion and attempts judgement\n• Provides correct graph and provides correct concentration values\nOR\n• Provides a graph with some correct features, provides concentration\nvalues and applies conversion\n4\n• Provides correct graph\nOR\n• Provides a graph with some correct features and provides some\nconcentration values\n3\n• Provides a substantially correct graph\nOR\n• Provides a graph with two correct features and extracts some relevant\nsample data\nOR\n2\n• Provides a graph with one correct feature, extracts some relevant sample\ndata and attempts a relevant conversion\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nConcentrations of metals ions in mol L–1\nSample Cu2+ × 10–5 mol L–1 Pb2+ × 10–5 mol L–1\nWater sample pre-treatment 5.95 4.75\nWater sample post-treatment 0.25 0.85\nThe copper and lead concentrations have both been lowered by the treatment. To compare\nthe post treatment concentrations to the discharge standard, the sample concentrations need\nto be converted to mg L–1.\nCu2+: 5.95 × 10–5 mol L–1 × 63.55 g mol–1 × 1000 mg g–1 = 3.78 mg L–1\n0.25 × 10–5 mol L–1 × 63.55 g mol–1 × 1000 mg g–1 = 0.20 mg L–1\nPb2+: 4.75 × 10–5 mol L–1 × 207.2 g mol–1 × 1000 mg g–1 = 9.84 mg L–1\n0.85 × 10–5 mol L–1 × 207.2 g mol–1 × 1000 mg g–1 = 1.8 mg L–1\nConclusion: The copper ion concentration has been successfully lowered to less than the\ndischarge limit. However, the lead ion concentration, although reduced, remains above the\ndischarge limit. The treatment is only partially successful.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q30","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":30,"section":"II","marks":3,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Static and Dynamic Equilibrium","iq":"What happens when chemical reactions do not go through to completion?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":3,"content":["Mod 5 Static and Dynamic Equilibrium"]}],"questionText":"Question 30 (3 marks)\nThe following data apply to magnesium fluoride and magnesium chloride dissolving\nin water at 298 K.\nMagnesium fluoride Magnesium chloride\nDsolH ° (kJ mol−1) −7.81 −160\nDsolS ° (J K–1 mol−1) −223 −115\nTDsolS ° (kJ mol−1) −66.4 −34.2\nDsolG ° (kJ mol−1) +58.6 −125\n̄\n̄\n̄\n̄\n3\nCompare the effects of enthalpy and entropy on the solubility of these salts.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 27 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n4074310368","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q30-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q30-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 30\nCriteria Marks\n• Compares the effects of enthalpy and entropy in relation to the solubility\nof both salts 3\n• Compares enthalpy and entropy values for both salts\n• Identifies the solubility of both salts\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nMagnesium chloride has a negative \u0001solG\u0001 (−125 kJ mol−1\n) and hence dissolves in water\nspontaneously, whereas the corresponding value for magnesium fluoride is +58.6 kJ mol−1\n,\nso magnesium fluoride does not dissolve in water spontaneously.\nBoth salts have a negative \u0001solS\u0001 ; both result in a positive −T\u0001solS\u0001 contribution to\n\u0001 . solG\u0001\nBoth salts have a negative \u0001sol H \u0001 , however magnesium chloride has a significantly\nnegative \u0001sol H \u0001 (−160 kJ mol−1\n) which is greater than the −T\u0001solS\u0001 contribution\n(+34.2 kJ mol−1\n), resulting in a negative \u0001solG\u0001 . On the other hand, magnesium fluoride\nhas a relatively small negative \u0001sol H \u0001 (−7.81 kJ mol−1\n) which is smaller than the\n−T\u0001sol S\u0001 contribution (+66.4 kJ mol−1\n), resulting in a positive \u0001solG\u0001 .","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q31","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":31,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)","iq":"How can the position of equilibrium be described and what does the equilibrium constant represent?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 5 Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)"]}],"questionText":"Question 31 (4 marks)\nThe following reaction occurs in an aqueous solution.\nHgCl 2−\n4 (aq) + Cu2+(aq) W CuCl 2−4 (aq) + Hg2+(aq) K = 4.55 × 10−11eq\nA solution containing a mixture of HgCl 2−\n4 (aq) and Cu2+(aq) ions is prepared. The\ninitial concentration of each ion is 0.100 mol L–1 and there are no other ions present.\nCalculate the concentration of Hg2+(aq) ions once the system has reached equilibrium.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 28 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n9566310369","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q31-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q31-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 31\nCriteria Marks\n• Calculates the concentration of Hg2+ ions showing all working 4\n• Provides substantially correct working 3\n• Provides some correct steps 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\n⎡CuCl 2– ⎤ ⎡ ⎤ ⎦⎥ Hg2+\n⎣⎢ 4 ⎣⎢ ⎦⎥ K = eq ⎡HgCl 2– ⎤ ⎡ ⎤ ⎦⎥ Cu2+\n⎣⎢ 4 ⎣⎢ ⎦⎥\nHgCl4\n⎡ 2–\n⎣⎢ ⎤⎦⎥ Cu ⎡ 2+⎣⎢ ⎤⎦⎥ CuCl4\n⎡ 2–\n⎣⎢ ⎤⎦⎥ Hg ⎡ 2+⎣⎢ ⎤⎦⎥\nI 0.100 0.100 0 0\nC −x −x +x +x\nE 0.100 − x 0.100 − x x x\n2x K = = 4.55 × 10−11\n(0.100 − x)\n2\nSolving for x (take square root OR assume that x ≤ 0.100 OR use the quadratic formula),\nx = 6.75 × 10−7mol L−1\nSo Hg ⎡ ⎤ 2+\n⎦⎥ = 6.75 × 10−7mol L−1\n⎣⎢","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q32","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":32,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 7 Alcohols"]}],"questionText":"Question 32 (4 marks)\nThiols are the sulfur analogues of alcohols in that the oxygen atom of the alcohol is\nreplaced by a sulfur atom. For example, methanethiol (CH3SH) is the analogue of\nmethanol (CH3OH). The boiling points of some straight chain alcohols and thiols are\ngiven in the following graph.\n7\n175\n150\n125\n100\n75\n50\n25\n0\n0123456\nCarbon atoms per molecule\nBoiling point (°C)\nCH3CH2SH\nCH3CH2OH\nAlcohols\nThiols\nExplain the patterns of the boiling points shown in the graph.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 29 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0008310361","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q32-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q32-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 32\nCriteria Marks\n• Explains patterns in the boiling points 4\n• Explains most of the patterns in the boiling points 3\n• Describes the patterns in the boiling point\nOR\n• Explains a pattern in the boiling points\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nBoiling points for both series increase with increasing number of carbon atoms. This is due to\ndispersion forces increasing with increasing chain length.\nThe boiling point of the alcohol is always higher than the analogous thiol. This is due to\nhydrogen bonding between alcohols being stronger than the dispersion forces between\nthiols.\nThe difference between the boiling points of analogous alcohols and thiols decreases with\nincreasing chain length. This is due to the hydrogen bonding having a smaller and smaller\ncontribution to total intermolecular forces as chain length increases.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q33","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":33,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 6 Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory"]}],"questionText":"Question 33 (4 marks)\nA student adds 1.17 g of Al(OH)3(s) to 0.500 L of 0.100 mol L–1 HCl(aq).\nCalculate the pH of the resulting solution. Assume that the volume of the resulting\nsolution is 0.500 L.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nPlease turn over\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 30 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n8952310367","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q33-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q33-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 33\nCriteria Marks\n• Calculates pH correctly showing all working 4\n• Provides substantially correct working 3\n• Provides some correct steps 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nAl(OH)3(s) + 3HCl(aq) → AlCl3(aq) + 3H2O(l)\n1.17 g n(Al(OH)3 ) = = 0.0150 mol\n78.004 g mol−1\nn(HCl) = 0.500 L × 0.100 mol L−1 = 0.0500 mol\nn(HCl reacting with Al(OH) ) = 3 × 0.150 mol L−1 = 0.0450 mol 3\nn(HCl in excess) = 0.0500 mol − 0.0450 mol = 0.0050 mol\n0.0050 mol [HCl] = = 0.010 mol L−1\n0.500 L\npH = −log10(0.010) = 2.00","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2019-q34","subject":"Chemistry","year":2019,"questionNumber":34,"section":"II","marks":7,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-2","12-4","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":7,"content":["Mod 7 Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases"]}],"questionText":"Question 34 (7 marks)\nThe following reaction scheme can be used to synthesise ethyl ethanoate.\nC\nH\nH O\nH C OH\nStep 1 Step 2\nStep 3\nC\nH\nH\nH\nH\nC C H\nH\nH O\nH C O\nD\nC\nC\nH\nH\nH\nH\nH C OH\nB\nC\nH\nH\nH\nH\nH C Cl\nA\nOutline the reagents and conditions required for each step and how the product of each\nstep could be identified.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n7\nEnd of paper\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 31 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2306310364","questionImages":["/img/c2019-q34-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2019-q34-mg0.webp","/img/c2019-q34-mg1.webp"],"mgText":"Question 34\nCriteria Marks\n• Outlines the appropriate reagents and conditions\n• Outlines differences in chemical reactivity or spectroscopic data for\nproducts of all three steps\n7\n• Outlines the appropriate reagents and conditions\n• Outlines differences in chemical reactivity or spectroscopic data for\nproducts of two steps\n6\n• Outlines some appropriate reagents and conditions\n• Outlines chemical reactivity or spectroscopic data for the products of\nsome steps\n4–5\n• Outlines an appropriate reagent and/or condition\nAND/OR\n• Outlines chemical reactivity or spectroscopic data for the products\n2–3\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nIn step 1, chloroethane (A) can be converted to ethanol (B) by heating with dilute aqueous\npotassium hydroxide. The mass spectrum of ethanol will have a single molecular ion peak at\nm/z = 46, whereas chloroethane will give two peaks at m/z 64 and 66.\nIn step 2, ethanol (B) can be converted to ethanoic acid (C) using a strong oxidant, for\nexample: acidified potassium permanganate. A C=O peak will appear in the IR spectrum\n(1680–1750 cm–1) or in the 13C NMR spectrum (160–185 ppm); ethanol will show neither\npeak.\nIn step 3, ethyl ethanoate (D) can be synthesised by heating a mixture of ethanol, ethanoic\nacid and concentrated sulfuric acid under reflux. The 1\nH NMR spectrum of ethyl ethanoate\nwill have 3 signals, whereas both ethanol and ethanoic acid will have 2.\nAnswers could include:\nEthanol (B) can be identified in a number of ways, for example:\n•\t Reactivity tests eg turns acidified potassium dichromate solution from orange to green, or\ndecolourises acidified potassium permanganate solution from purple. Also gives a red\ncolour with ceric ammonium nitrate, or gives hydrogen gas with sodium. Chloroethane\nreacts with none of these reagents.\n• An O−H peak will appear in the IR spectrum (broad peak at 3230–3550 cm−1\n) and in the\n1\nH NMR spectrum (variable chemical shift, 1–6 ppm, concentration-dependent); the IR\nspectrum of chloroethane will not contain this peak.\n1 • The H NMR spectrum of ethanol will have 3 signals, whereas that of chloroethane will\nhave 2.\n•\t The mass spectrum of ethanol will have a single molecular ion peak at m/z = 46, whereas\nchloroethane will give two peaks at m/z 64 and 66.\nIn step 2, ethanol (B) can be converted to ethanoic acid (C) using a strong oxidant, for\nexample: Jones’ reagent (CrO3 in H2SO4), and many others.\nEthanoic acid (C) can be identified in a number of ways, for example:\n•\t Indicators (eg blue litmus turning red), carbon dioxide given off when reacted with sodium\ncarbonate or sodium hydrogencarbonate. Ethanol will do neither.\n•\t C=O peak will appear in the IR spectrum (1680–1750 cm−1\n) and in the 13C NMR\nspectrum (160 – 185 ppm); ethanol will show neither peak.\nThe 1 • H NMR spectrum of ethanoic acid will not have a peak in 3.3–4.8 ppm range; the\n13C NMR spectrum will not have a peak in the 50–90 ppm range. Ethanol will have a peak\nin both ranges due to the CH2 group.\n1 • The H NMR spectrum of ethanoic acid will have 2 signals, whereas that of ethanol will\nhave 3.\n•\t The mass spectrum of ethanoic acid will have a molecular ion peak at m/z = 60, whereas\nthat of ethanol is at m/z 46.\nEthyl ethanoate (D) can be identified in a number of ways, for example:\n•\t Ethyl ethanoate will have no O−H peaks in the IR spectrum (broad peak at 3230–3550\ncm−1\n) and the 1H NMR spectrum (variable chemical shift, 1–6 ppm, concentration\u0002dependent). These peaks are present in the spectra of ethanol and ethanoic acid.\n•\t The mass spectrum of ethyl ethanoate will have a molecular ion peak at m/z = 102;\nethanoic acid will have a molecular ion peak at m/z = 60 and ethanol at m/z 46.\n•\t Ethyl ethanoate has a distinct smell.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q1","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":1,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"1 What is the function of the magnetic field in a mass spectrometer?\nA. It detects the mass of the particles.\nB. It deflects the stream of charged particles.\nC. It excites electrons to higher energy levels.\nD. It produces a stream of electrons that bombards the sample.","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q1-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2020-q2","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":2,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Properties of Acids and Bases","iq":"What is an acid and what is a base?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"2 Which indicator in the table would be best for distinguishing between a face cleanser\n(pH =5.0) and a soap (pH = 9.0)?\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.\nIndicator Colour (low pH – high pH) pH range\nBromophenol blue Yellow – blue 3.0 − 4.6\nMethyl orange Red – yellow 3.1− 4.4\nPhenol red Yellow – red 6.4 − 8.0\nThymolphthalein Colourless – blue 9.4 −10.6","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q2-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2020-q3","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":3,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-4"],"parts":[],"questionText":"3 Which of the following compounds is the most basic?\nA. Ethane\nB. Ethanol\nC. Ethanamine\nD. Ethyl ethanoate\n– 3 –","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q3-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2020-q4","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":4,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"4 Which pair of compounds would be difficult to distinguish using infrared spectroscopy?\nA. Butane and propane\nB. Ethane and propan-1-ol\nC. Propanol and propanoic acid\nD. Methanamine and propanone","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q4-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2020-q5","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":5,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Nomenclature","iq":"How do we systematically name organic chemical compounds?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-15"],"parts":[],"questionText":"5 A 13C NMR spectrum is shown.\n40 35 30 25 20\nChemical shift (ppm)\n15 10 5 0\nWhich compound gives rise to this spectrum?\nA. chloroethane\nB. 1-chloropropane\nC. 1,2-dichloroethane\nD. 1,2-dichloropropane\n– 4 –","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q5-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2020-q6","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":6,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Nomenclature","iq":"How do we systematically name organic chemical compounds?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"6 The structure of a compound is shown.\nBr\nBr C\nF\nH\nC\nH\nOH\nC H\nBr\nWhat is the preferred IUPAC name of this compound?\nA. 1,1,1-tribromo-2-fluoropropan-3-ol\nB. 2-fluoro-3,3,3-tribromopropan-1-ol\nC. 2-fluoro-1,1,1-tribromopropan-3-ol\nD. 3,3,3-tribromo-2-fluoropropan-1-ol","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q6-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2020-q7","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":7,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Nomenclature","iq":"How do we systematically name organic chemical compounds?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"7 The structures of four isomers are shown.\nH\nH\nCH\nH\nH\nC\nO\nC\nH\nH\nC OH\nH\nCC\nOH\nH\nC\nH\nH\nC OH\nH\nH\nH\nH\nCH\nH\nH\nC\nH\nH\nC O\nO\nC H\nH\nH\nCH\nO\nC\nH\nH\nC\nH\nH\nC OH\nCompound 1 Compound 2\nCompound 3 Compound 4\nWhich statement is correct?\nA. Compounds 1 and 2 are chain isomers.\nB. Compounds 1 and 4 are chain isomers.\nC. Compounds 2 and 3 are functional group isomers.\nD. Compounds 2 and 4 are positional isomers.\n– 5 –","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q7-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2020-q8","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":8,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"8 A weak base is titrated with 1.0 mol L−1 aqueous HCl. The pH curve is shown.\n2\n4\n6\n8\n10\n12\nVolume of acid added (mL)\npH\n0\n0\n20 40 60 80 100\nAt which pH value would the solution be most effective as a buffer?\nA. 5\nB. 7\nC. 8\nD. 9\n– 6 –","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q8-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2020-q9","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":9,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"9 Which compound reacts readily with sodium hydrogen carbonate?\nCH3\nCH3\nA. 3C CH\nO\nC OH\nCH3\nOH\nC. 3C CH CH2 CH3\nCH3\nCH3\n3C CH\nO\nB. C CH3\nCH3\nCH3\nD. 3C CH CH2 CH2 CH2 OH","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q9-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2020-q10","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":10,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Properties of Acids and Bases","iq":"What is an acid and what is a base?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"10 Equimolar solutions of NaCl(aq), NH4Cl(aq) and NaCH3COO(aq) were prepared.\nIn which of the following are these salt solutions listed from least to most acidic?\nA. NaCl(aq), NH4Cl(aq), NaCH3COO(aq)\nB. NaCl(aq), NaCH COO(aq 3 ), NH4Cl(aq)\nC. NH4Cl(aq), NaCl(aq), NaCH3COO(aq)\nD. NaCH3COO(aq), NaCl(aq), NH4Cl(aq)\n– 7 –","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q10-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2020-q11","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":11,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Solution Equilibria","iq":"How does solubility relate to chemical equilibrium?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-2"],"parts":[],"questionText":"11 Equal volumes of two 0.04 mol L−1 solutions were mixed together.\nWhich pair of solutions would give the greatest mass of precipitate?\nA. Ba(OH)2 and MgCl2\nB. Ba(OH)2 and MgSO4\nC. Ba(OH)2 and NaCl\nD. Ba(OH)2 and Na2SO4","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q11-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2020-q12","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":12,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Polymers","iq":"What are the properties and uses of polymers?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"12 The structure of part of a polymer chain is shown.\nC\nO\nO\nO\nH\nH\nC\nH\nn\nH\nC\nO\nC\nWhich statement best explains why plastics made from this polymer require a temperature\nof approximately 250°C before they begin to soften?\nA. The carbon–carbon bonds in the polymer chains are strong.\nB. The carbon–hydrogen bonds in the polymer chains are strong.\nC. Extensive dipole–dipole and dispersion forces exist between the polymer chains.\nD. Extensive hydrogen bonds and dispersion forces exist between the polymer chains.","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q12-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2020-q13","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":13,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Hydrocarbons","iq":"How can hydrocarbons be classified based on their structure and reactivity?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"13 Which of the following conversions results in the formation of a different shape around\nthe carbon atom?\nA. Methanoic acid to methanal\nB. Methanoic acid to methanol\nC. Methanoic acid to methanamide\nD. Methanoic acid to sodium methanoate\n– 8 –","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q13-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2020-q14","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":14,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"},{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)","iq":"How can the position of equilibrium be described and what does the equilibrium constant represent?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-13","12-2"],"parts":[],"questionText":"14 The equation for the autoionisation of water is shown.\n2H O(l ) W H O+\n2 3 (aq) + OH−(aq)\nAt 50°C the water ionisation constant, Kw , is 5.5 × 10−14.\nWhat is the pH of water at 50°C?\nA. 5.50\nB. 6.63\nC. 6.93\nD. 7.00","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q14-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2020-q15","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":15,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"15 The structure of chloroacetamide is shown.\nC\nO\nC\nH\nN\nH\nH\nH\nCl\nThe common isotopes of chlorine are 35Cl and 37Cl.\nThe mass spectrum of chloroacetamide contains a peak at m /z = 51.\nWhat is the most likely source of this peak?\nA. OCl\n+\nB. NH2\n+ C. C4H3\n+\nD. CH2Cl\n– 9 –","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q15-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2020-q16","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":16,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Factors that Affect Equilibrium","iq":"What factors affect equilibrium and how?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"16 Compounds X, Y and Z are in equilibrium. The diagram shows the effects of temperature\nand pressure on the equilibrium yield of compound Z.\n10 MPa\n30 MPa\n50 MPa\nTemperature\nYield\nWhich equation would be consistent with this data?\nA. X(g) + 3Y(g) W 2Z(g) DH > 0\nB. X(g) + 3Y(g) W 2Z(g) DH < 0\nC. 2X(g) W 2Y(g) + Z(g) DH > 0\nD. 2X(g) W 2Y(g) + Z(g) DH < 0","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q16-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2020-q17","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":17,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Solution Equilibria","iq":"How does solubility relate to chemical equilibrium?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"17 The following apparatus was set up in a temperature-controlled laboratory.\nBeaker\nCuSO4(s)\nSaturated CuSO4(aq)\nLid\nExcess solid sodium hydroxide is added to the beaker.\nWhich row of the table correctly identifies the change in the CuSO4(s) mass and the\ncolour of the solution after several days?\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.\nSolid CuSO4 mass Colour of solution\nNo change No change\nNo change Blue colour fades\nDecreases Blue colour intensifies\nDecreases Blue colour fades\n– 10 –","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q17-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2020-q18","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":18,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"18 An aqueous solution of sodium hydrogen carbonate has a pH greater than 7.\nWhich statement best explains this observation?\nA. H O(l) is a stronger acid than HCO −(aq 2 3 ).\nB. HCO −\n3 (aq) is a weaker acid than H2CO (aq 3 ).\nC. Na+(aq) reacts with water to produce the strong base NaOH(aq).\nD. The conjugate acid of HCO −\n3 (aq) is a stronger acid than H2O(l ).","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q18-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2020-q19","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":19,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Factors that Affect Equilibrium","iq":"What factors affect equilibrium and how?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-4"],"parts":[],"questionText":"19 Nitrogen dioxide reacts to form dinitrogen tetroxide in a sealed flask according to the\nfollowing equation.\n2NO (g) W N O (g 2 2 4 ) DH = −57.2 kJ mol−1\nWhich graph best represents the rates of both the forward and reverse reactions when an\nequilibrium system containing these gases is cooled at time t ? Rate\nA.\nRate\nB.\nt t\nTime Time\nRate\nC.\nRate\nD.\nt t\nTime Time\nForward reaction Reverse reaction\n– 11 –","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q19-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2020-q20","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":20,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Solution Equilibria","iq":"How does solubility relate to chemical equilibrium?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-4"],"parts":[],"questionText":"20 The graph shows the concentration of silver and chromate ions which can exist in a\nsaturated solution of silver chromate.\n10\n15\n20\n25\n30\n35\n40\n45\n0\n5\n0 2345678 1\nCrO42−(aq) × 10−5 mol L−1\nAg+(aq) × 10−4 mol L−1\nBased on the information provided, what is the K\nsp for silver chromate?\nA. 1.1 × 10−8\nB. 2.2 × 10−8\nC. 1.1 × 10−12\nD. 4.4 × 10−12\nBLANK PAGE\n© 2020 NSW Education Standards Authority\n– 12 –\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 13 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2020 HIGHER SCHOOL CERTIFICATE EXAMINATION\nChemistry\nSection II Answer Booklet\n80 marks\nAttempt Questions 21–36\nAllow about 2 hours and 25 minutes for this section\nInstructions • Write your Centre Number and Student Number at the top of this\npage.\n• Answer the questions in the spaces provided. These spaces\nprovide guidance for the expected length of response.\n• Show all relevant working in questions involving calculations.\n• Extra writing space is provided at the back of this booklet.\nIf you use this space, clearly indicate which question you are\nanswering.\nPlease turn over\nCentre Number\nStudent Number\n1041\n2690310371\n15050\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 14 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\nBLANK PAGE\n6580310373\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 15 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2043310373","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q20-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2020-q21","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":21,"section":"II","marks":2,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":2,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Organic Substances"]}],"questionText":"Question 21 (2 marks)\nThe mass spectrum of an alkane is shown.\n10\n0\n20\n40\n60\n80\n100\n20 30\nm/z\nRelative intensity\n40 50\nUse the information provided to identify the alkane and justify your choice.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n2\nPlease turn over\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 16 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2237310378","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q21-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q21-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 21\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nCorrectly identifies the alkane\nProvides a valid justification 2\n•\nOR\nCorrectly identifies the alkane\n1\n• Shows some understanding of the mass spectrum\nSample answer:\nThe alkane is propane. This can be determined from the mass spectrum as the parent ion\nhas a molecular mass of 44, which is consistent with propane C3H8.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q22","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":22,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"},{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Factors that Affect Equilibrium","iq":"What factors affect equilibrium and how?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-15","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":5,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Inorganic Substances","Mod 5 Factors that Affect Equilibrium"]}],"questionText":"Question 22 (5 marks)\nA 0.1 mol L−1 solution of an unknown salt is to be analysed. The cation is one of\nmagnesium, calcium or barium. The anion is one of chloride, acetate or hydroxide.\nOutline a sequence of tests that could be performed in a school laboratory to confirm\nthe identity of this salt solution. Include expected observations and a balanced\nchemical equation in your answer.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n5\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 17 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n1575310372","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q22-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q22-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 22\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nOutlines a sequence of suitable tests with expected observations\nIncludes a balanced chemical equation\n5\n• Outlines a sequence of suitable tests and most of the expected\nobservations 4\n• Includes a substantially correct balanced chemical equation\n•\n•\nProvides suitable tests that can identify cation (s) and anion(s) present\nIncludes some expected observations and/or a balanced chemical\nequation\n3\n•\nOR\nProvides one test and observation that can identify a cationOR anion\npresent\n• Provides tests that can identify cations and anions present\nOR\n2\n• Provides a balanced chemical equation and one test that can identify a\ncation OR anion present\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nCation\nCarry out a flame test\n– If pale green → barium\n– If brick red → calcium\n– No colour → magnesium\nAnion\n1. Add Cu(NO ) 3 2\n(aq)\nIf pale blue precipitate forms → OH– present\n2. Add AgNO3(aq)\n– If white ppt forms → Cl– present\n– If no ppt forms → CH3COO– present\n+ − – Ag (aq) + Cl (aq) → AgCl (s)\nAnswers could include:\nTest pH with universal indicator, pH meter\nCl–\n→ neutral\nCH3COO–\n→ slightly basic\nOH–\n→ very basic","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q23","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":23,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Chemical Synthesis and Design","iq":"What are the implications for society of chemical synthesis and design?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-5","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 8 Chemical Synthesis and Design"]}],"questionText":"Question 23 (4 marks)\nThe flow chart summarises an industrial process for the synthesis of ethane-1, 2-diol.\nReactor 1\n200–300°C\ncatalyst\nSeparator 1\nSeparator 2\nEthylene\noxide\nReactor 2\n50–70°C\ncatalyst\nLiquid\nethane-1,2-diol\nLiquid\nby-products\nFurther\nprocessing for sale\nTransport to\nmarkets\nWater\nEthene Oxygen\nEthene and\noxygen\nExplain THREE factors that may have been considered in the design of this industrial\nprocess. Make specific reference to the flow chart.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 18 –\nOT write in this area.\n0125310372","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q23-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q23-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 23\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nExplains three relevant factors\nMakes specific reference to the flow chart 4\n•\nOR\nExplains two relevant factors with some reference to the flow chart\n• Explains one relevant factor and outlines two other relevant factors with\nsome reference to the flow chart 3\nOR\n• Explains three relevan t factors without specific reference to the flow chart\n• Explains one relevant factor\nOR 2\n• Outlines two relevant factors\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nThe use of a catalyst in reactions 1 and 2 allows a faster rate at a lower temperature. This\nboth increases the efficiency of the process and reduces energy consumption that makes the\nprocess more economical and ultimately less polluting.\nUnused reactant gases are recycled after being separated from the reaction mixture in\nseparator 1. This means that resources are not wasted – making the process more\neconomical as less reactant needs to be purchased from suppliers.\nMarkets have been accessed for the major product (ethane-1,2-diol) – without a market the\nindustrial process is not economically viable – the location of these markets has been\ndetermined and suitable transport arranged – the industrial plant has probably been located\nnear a major port, rail or road network to facilitate economical and rapid transport to markets.\nAnswers could include:\n•\t By-products are also produced rather than wastes to be disposed of. These by-products\nare sent to markets, which indicates that all the products of the reaction have a purpose.\nThis makes the entire process more economical and less wasteful (potentially 100% atom\neconomy).\n•\t Access to reactants – the plant would be located so that ethylene and oxygen gases\nwould be easily accessible – perhaps near a petrochemical plant or road/rail network so\nthat these resources could be transported easily and cheaply.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q24","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":24,"section":"II","marks":10,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Products of Reactions Involving Hydrocarbons","iq":"What are the products of reactions of hydrocarbons and how do they react?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":1,"content":["Mod 7 Alcohols"]},{"part":"b","marks":3,"content":["Mod 7 Products of Reactions Involving Hydrocarbons"]},{"part":"c","marks":2,"content":["Mod 7 Alcohols"]},{"part":"d","marks":4,"content":["Mod 7 Alcohols"]}],"questionText":"Question 24 (10 marks)\nBiodiesel, an alternative fuel to diesel, may be produced from vegetable oil. The\nchemical reaction which converts oils from biomass into biodiesel is shown. R1, R2\nand R3 are alkyl chains which may vary from 10 to 22 carbons in length.\nO\nCH2 O C R1\nO\nCH O C R2\nO\nCH2 O C R3\nCH2 OH\nCH OH\nCH2 OH\nO\nC2H5 O C R1\nO\nC2H5 O C R2\nO\nC2H5 O C R3\nVegetable oil Biodiesel Glycerol\n+ 3C2H5OH +\nNaOH\n(a) Which functional group is present in both the oil and the biodiesel?\n...............................................................................................................................\n1\n(b) Explain why biodiesel (C14H30O2) produces less soot than diesel (C18H38)\nwhen combusted under the same conditions. Support your answer with balanced\nchemical equations.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n3\nQuestion 24 continues on page 19\nDo N\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 19 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n4973310377\nQuestion 24 (continued)\n(c) The energy densities of biodiesel and diesel are 38 MJ kg–1 and 43 MJ kg–1\nrespectively. The densities of biodiesel and diesel are 0.90 kg L–1 and 0.83 kg L–1\nrespectively.\nWhen 60.0 L of diesel is combusted in a typical engine, 2141 MJ of energy is\nreleased.\nWhat volume of biodiesel would be required to produce the same amount of\nenergy?\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\n(d) Explain TWO advantages and TWO disadvantages of using bioethanol (ethanol\nproduced from biomass) as an alternative to a fossil fuel.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n4\nEnd of Question 24\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 20 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0279310372","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q24-0.webp","/img/c2020-q24-1.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q24-mg0.webp","/img/c2020-q24-mg1.webp","/img/c2020-q24-mg3.webp","/img/c2020-q24-mg5.webp"],"mgText":"Question 24 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly identifies the functional group 1\nSample answer:\nEster\nQuestion 24 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nExplains why biodiesel produces less soot than diesel\nSupports answer with relevant and correctly balanced chemical equations\n(states NOT required)\n3\n• Makes use of at lease one substantially correct balanced equation to\nprovide an explanation\nOR 2\n• Provides two correctly balanced equations\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\n55 C H + O → 18CO + 19H O 18 38 2 2 2 2\n41\nC H O + O → 14CO + 15H O 14 30 2 2 2 2 2\nMore supplied oxygen is required for the complete combustion of diesel compared to\nbiodiesel. Therefore it is more likely that diesel will combust incompletely, producing soot.\n41 Eg C H + O → 10CO + 2CO + 6C + 19H O 18 38 2 2 2 2\nQuestion 24 (c)\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly calculates the volume of biodiesel 2\n• Provides acorrect step 1\nSample answer:\nEnergy density (biodiesel) = 38 MJ kg−1\n2141\nm (biodiesel) needed = = 56.3 kg 38\nd (biodiesel) = 0.90 kg L−1\n56.3 V (biodiesel) = = 63 L\n0.90\nQuestion 24 (d)\nCriteria Marks\n• Explains TWO advantages and TWO disadvantages of using bioethanol\nas an alternative to a fossil fuel 4\n• Outlines TWO advantages and TWO disadvantages of using bioethanol\nas an alternative to a fossil fuel 3\n• Explains at least TWO of them\n• Outlines TWO advantages and TWO disadvantages of using bioethanol\nas an alternative to a fossil fuel\nOR\n• Explains TWO advantages orTWO disadvantages of using bioethanol as\nan alternative to a fossil fuel 2\nOR\n• Explains ONE advantage and O NE disadvantage of using bioeth anol as\nan alternative to a fossil fuel\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nBioethanol is produced from renewable resources while petrol is produced from non\u0002renewable crude oil reserves. Thus, ethanol is a sustainable fuel, which may be continually\nproduced while petrol is unsustainable. Combustion of petrol increases greenhouse gas\nconcentrations in the atmosphere. If green energy is used to produce bioethanol, the carbon\ndioxide released during the combustion may be removed by plants during photosynthesis,\nresulting in a carbon neutral process. Increased demand for ethanol to replace petrol may\ncause crops to be grown for fuel rather than food. Food shortages or increased food prices\nmay result. As more crops are grown to produce biofuels, more fertiliser is used. The excess\nuse of fertilisers can result in soil erosion and can lead to land and water pollution.\nAnswers could include:\nAdvantages of bioethanol over petrol\n•\t Ethanol produces less particulates, which can be responsible for significant lung disease\nand may even be associated with the formation of cancer. Reducing airborne pollutants\nalso has the added benefit of reducing the cost of health care in a community.\n•\t Ethanol is biodegradable. Thus, spills pose less of an environmental threat than spills of\nnon-biodegradable petrol, which can cause long-term contamination of soil and water\nbodies.\nDisadvantages of bioethanol\n•\t The use of water to produce more crops to produce bioethanol can put pressure on local\nwater resources. In areas where there is water scarcity, production of crops to be used in\nmaking bioethanol compromises water security for this region – possibly leading to health\nissues due to lack of potable water.\n•\t Energy is needed for the mechanised sowing, fertilising and harvesting of crops.\nSignificant amounts of energy are required for the distillation of ethanol from fermentation\nmixtures. If fossil fuels are used as the source of this energy then the use of bioethanol as\nan alternative would not be carbon neutral and would contribute to global warming.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q25","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":25,"section":"II","marks":7,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Properties of Acids and Bases","iq":"What is an acid and what is a base?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":7,"content":["Mod 6 Properties of Acids and Bases"]}],"questionText":"Question 25 (7 marks)\nCitric acid reacts with sodium hydroxide according to the following chemical equation:\nC6H8O7(aq) + 3NaOH(aq) Na3C6H5O7(aq) + 3H2O(l ) .\nVarious volumes of 1.0 mol L−1 citric acid solution were mixed with 8.0 mL of a sodium\nhydroxide solution of unknown concentration and sufficient deionised water added to make\nthe total volume of the resulting solution 14.0 mL. The change in temperature of each solution\nwas measured.\nThe data are given in the table.\nVolume of 1.0 mol L−1 Temperature\ncitric acid (aq) increase\n(mL) (°C)\n0.0 0.00\n1.0 2.50\n2.0 5.20\n3.0 6.15\n4.0 6.10\n5.0 6.20\n6.0 6.15\nQuestion 25 continues on page 21\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 21 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n6089310377\nQuestion 25 (continued)\nBy graphing the data in the table and performing relevant calculations, determine the\nconcentration of the sodium hydroxide solution.\n7\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\nEnd of Question 25\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 22 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2178310372","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q25-0.webp","/img/c2020-q25-1.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q25-mg0.webp","/img/c2020-q25-mg2.webp"],"mgText":"Question 25\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nProvides graph with\n– correctly labelled axes including units\n– appropriate scales\n– correctly plotted points\n– correct line(s) of best fit\nCorrectly calculates the concentration of sodium hydroxide with units\n7\n• Provides a correct graph and most of the steps for calculating the\nconcentration of sodium hydroxide 6\n• Provides a substan tially correct graph and atleast one step for calculating\nthe concentration of sodium hydroxide 4–5\n• Provides a graph with some correct features\nAND/OR\n• Provides some steps for calculating the concentration of sodium\nhydroxide\n2–3\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nQuestion 25 (continued)\nSample answer:\nn(citric acid at equivalence point) = 2.4 × 10–3 L × 1.0 mol L–1 = 2.4 × 10–3 mol\nn(sodium hydroxide at equivalence point) = 2.4 × 10–3 mol × 3 = 7.2 × 10–3 mol\n[sodium hydroxide] = 7.2 × 10–3 mol ÷ 8.0 × 10–3 L = 0.90 mol L–1","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q26","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":26,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Static and Dynamic Equilibrium","iq":"What happens when chemical reactions do not go through to completion?"},{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Factors that Affect Equilibrium","iq":"What factors affect equilibrium and how?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-4","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":1,"content":["Mod 5 Factors that Affect Equilibrium"]},{"part":"b","marks":4,"content":["Mod 5 Static and Dynamic Equilibrium"]}],"questionText":"Question 26 (5 marks)\nNitric oxide gas (NO) can be produced from the direct combination of nitrogen gas\nand oxygen gas in a reversible reaction.\n(a) Write the balanced chemical equation for this reaction.\n...............................................................................................................................\n1\n(b) The energy profile diagram for this reaction is shown. Enthalpy (kJ mol–1)\nReaction coordinate\nN2, O2\nNO\nExplain, using collision theory, how an increase in temperature would affect the\nvalue of K for this system. Refer to the diagram in your answer. eq\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 23 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n4072310377","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q26-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q26-mg0.webp","/img/c2020-q26-mg1.webp"],"mgText":"Question 26 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Writes a substantially correct balanced equation, including states 1\nSample answer:\nN2(g) + O2(g) ! 2NO(g)\nQuestion 26 (b)\n⎡\n⎣\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nProvides an explanation using collision theory\nRefers to the diagram\n4\n• Shows a sound understanding of how increasing temperature favours an\nendothermic process with reference to some aspects of the collision\ntheory\n3\n• Shows understanding of the effect of increasing temperature 2\n• Provides some relevant i nformation 1\nSample answer:\nThe forward reaction is endothermic. For the reaction to proceed the reacting molecules\nmust possess the activation energy to result in successful collisions. The activation energy for\nthe forward reaction is greater than that of the reverse, exothermic reaction. An increase in\ntemperature will increase the average kinetic energy of all molecules, resulting in more\neffective collisions increasing the reaction rate for both the forward and reverse reactions.\nHowever, the added temperature will have a greater impact on the forward reaction and the\nrate of this reaction will be higher than that of the reverse reaction.\n⎡\n⎣NO⎤⎦\n2\nAs a result, the forward reaction is favoured. As K =\n, a shift to the right will result\nN2 ⎡\n⎣\n⎤\n⎦ O ⎤\n2 ⎦\nin an increase in K.\nAnswers could include:\nReference to the Maxwell–Boltzmann distribution.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q27","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":27,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)","iq":"How can the position of equilibrium be described and what does the equilibrium constant represent?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"},{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-13","12-14","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":2,"content":["Mod 7 Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","Mod 6 Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory"]},{"part":"b","marks":3,"content":["Mod 5 Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)"]}],"questionText":"Question 27 (5 marks)\nA student makes up a solution of propan-2-amine in water with a concentration of\n1.00 mol L–1.\n(a) Using structural formulae, complete the equation for the reaction of\npropan-2-amine with water.\n2\n+ H2O W + OH−\n(b) The equilibrium constant for the reaction of propan-2-amine with water is\n4.37 × 10−4.\nCalculate the concentration of hydroxide ions in this solution.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n3\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 24 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n9362310374","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q27-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q27-mg0.webp","/img/c2020-q27-mg1.webp"],"mgText":"Question 27 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Provides correct structural formula for each organic species 2\n• Provides one correct structural formula or shows some understanding of\nprotonation 1\nSample answer:\nQuestion 27 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly calculates the concentration of hydroxide ions 3\n• Provides the main steps of the calculation 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nC H NH 3 7 2 + H O2 ! C H NH 3 7 3\n+ + OH–\nI 1.00 0 0\nC –x x x\nE 1.00 – x x x\nKb = ⎣ 3 7 3 ⎦ OH ⎡ – C H NH + ⎤⎡\n⎣ ⎤⎦\n⎡\n⎣\nC H NH ⎤ 3 7 2 ⎦\n= x2\n1.00 – x\nAssuming x ≪ 1.00, x2\n1.00 = 4.37 × 10–4\nx = 4.37 × 10–4 = 0.0207 mol L–1\n⎡ ⎤ OH− x = 0.0207 mol L–1\n⎣⎢ ⎦⎥ =","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q28","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":28,"section":"II","marks":3,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-2","12-3","12-5"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":3,"content":["Mod 6 Quantitative Analysis"]}],"questionText":"Question 28 (3 marks)\nA chemist used the following method to determine the concentration of a dilute\nsolution of propanoic acid (pKa = 4.88).\nThe chemist weighed out 1.000 g of solid NaOH on an electronic balance and then\nmade up the solution in a 250.0 mL volumetric flask.\nThe chemist then performed titrations, using bromocresol green as the indicator. This\nindicator is yellow below pH 3.2 and green above pH 5.2 .\nThe results are shown in the table.\nTitre Volume of NaOH(aq) added (mL)\n1 16.35\n2 10.10\n3 12.35\n4 11.25\nExplain why this method produces inaccurate and unreliable results.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n3\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 25 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n9827310370","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q28-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q28-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 28\nCriteria Marks\n• Explains why the results produced by the method are not accurate and\nnot reliable 3\n• Shows some understanding of why the results produced by the method\nare not accurate and/or not reliable 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nNaOH is used as a primary standard, but it is not a primary standard. Solid NaOH is\ndeliquescent, so the mass obtained by the balance is that of NaOH and water. The mass of\nNaOH is therefore lower than reported, and the solution made up is therefore lower in\nconcentration than expected, resulting in a titre that is larger than it should be, giving an\ninaccurate result.\nThe indicator chosen is inappropriate. It changes colour in the flat region of the titration\ncurve, prior to the equivalence point. Small titre volume differences will result in big variations\nin the observed endpoint, explaining the lack of reliability in titres 2, 3 and 4.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q29","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":29,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":5,"content":["Mod 7 Alcohols"]}],"questionText":"Question 29 (5 marks)\nThe flow chart shows reactions involving five different organic compounds, A to E .\nZnCl2 and concentrated HCl(aq)\nConcentrated\nH2SO4\nHCl(g\nConcentrated\n)\nH2SO4\nHCl(g)\nC4H9OH\nC4H8\nC4H9OH\nK2Cr2O7 + H+\nNo reaction\nA\nB\nC\nD\nE\nDraw the structure of each compound A to E in the corresponding space provided.\nA B\nC D\nE\n5\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 26 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0660310376","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q29-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q29-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 29\nCriteria Marks\n• Draws correct structural formulae for all five compounds 5\n• Draws correct structural formulae for four compounds\nOR\n• Draws five correct structural formulae with one minor error\n4\n• Draws substantially correct structural formulae demonstrating an\nunderstanding of most reactions 3\n• Draws structural formulae demonstrating an understanding of some\ndifferent reactions 2\n• Draws a substantially correct structural formula 1\nSample answer:","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q30","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":30,"section":"II","marks":7,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-1","12-15","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":7,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Organic Substances"]}],"questionText":"Question 30 (7 marks)\nA chemist discovered a bottle simply labelled ‘C5H10O2’.\nTo confirm the molecular structure of the contents of the bottle, a sample was submitted for\nanalysis by infrared spectroscopy and 1H and 13C NMR spectroscopy. The resulting spectra\nare shown.\n4000 3000 2000\nWavenumber (cm−1)\n1500 1000 500\n0\n50\n100\nTransmittance (%)\nInfrared spectrum\n100 80 60 40 20 0\nChemical shift (ppm)\n180 160 140 120\n23 21\n(ppm)\n13C NMR spectrum\n5.5 4.5 4.0 3.5 3.0 2.5 2.0 1.5 1.0 0.5 5.0\nChemical shift (ppm)\n(ppm)\n5.00\n4.95\n1.25\n1.20\n(ppm)\n1.00 3.00 6.00\n1H NMR spectrum\nQuestion 30 continues on page 27\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 27 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n7551310372\nQuestion 30 (continued)\nData from 1H NMR spectrum\nChemical shift Relative peak area Splitting pattern\n1.2 6 doublet (2)\n2.0 3 singlet (1)\n5.0 1 septet (7)\n1H NMR chemical shift data\nType of proton d ∕ ppm\nSi ( CH3) (TMS) 0\n4\nR CH3 0.7−1.3\nR CH2 R 1.2−1.5\nR CHR2 1.5−2.0\nH3C CO ( aldehydes, ketones or esters) 2.0−2.5\nCH CO ( aldehydes, ketones or esters) 2.1−2.6\nH3C O ( alcohols or esters) 3.2−4.0\nCH O ( alcohols or esters) 3.3−5.1\nR2 CH2 O (alcohols or esters) 3.5−5.0\nR OH 1−6\nR2C CHR (alkene) 4.5−7.0\nR CHO (aldehyde) 9.4−10.0\nR COOH 9.0−13.0\nQuestion 30 continues on page 28\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 28 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n3638310374\nQuestion 30 (continued)\nIn the space provided, draw a structural formula for the unknown compound that is\nconsistent with all of the information provided. Justify your answer with reference to\nthe information provided.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n7\nEnd of Question 30\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 29 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n6696310371","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q30-0.webp","/img/c2020-q30-1.webp","/img/c2020-q30-2.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q30-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 30\nCriteria Marks\n• Writes the correct structural formula of propan-2-yl acetate (naming not\nrequired)\n• Justifies the correct structure showing an extensive understanding of the\ninterpretation of spectroscopic data\n• Refers explicitly to the relevant spectroscopic data\n7\n• Writes the correct structural formula for an isomer of propan-2-yl acetate\n(methyl 2-methylpropanoate) (naming not required)\n• Justifies the structure showing a thorough understanding of the\ninterpretation of spectroscopic data\n• Refers to relevant spectroscopic data\n6\n• Shows a sound understanding of the interpretation of spectroscopic data\n• Uses relevant information presented in the question to justify the structure\nof the chemical\n• Provides a structural formula consistent with analysis\n4–5\n• Shows some understanding of the interpretation of spectroscopic data 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information 1\n•\t The IR spectrum shows a strong absorption at 1750 cm which is consistent with a\ncarbonyl group – this group is present in both esters and carboxylic acids\n•\t However, the lack of the broad O–H (acid) absorption band (stretch) at\n2500–3300 cm–1 means that it is not a carboxylic acid –COOH.\nThe 13C NMR Spectrum:\n•\t C atom double bonded to an O atom on the ester group: consistent with chemical shift\napproximately 170\n•\t C atom singly bonded to an O atom in an ester group: consistent with chemical shift\napproximately 70\n•\t C atom bonded to the C in an alkyl group: consistent with chemical shift approximately 20.\nThe 1H NMR Spectrum:\n•\t A septet – consistent with six neighbouring H atoms on two CH3 groups\n•\t A doublet – consistent with one neighbouring H atom\n•\t The septet and doublet combination is consistent with the presence of a –CH(CH3) 2\ngroup\n•\t A singlet – consistent with no neighbouring H atoms which would be produced by the\nisolated methyl group.\nThe spectroscopic data suggests that a CH(CH3) 2 group is attached to an O atom of the\nester group (that is, OCH(CH3) 2) and a CH3 group is attached to the C atom of the ester\ngroup (that is, CH3CO).\nAnswers could include:\nCondensed and skeletal formulae.\nSample answer:\nIR Spectrum:\n–1","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q31","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":31,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-4","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Inorganic Substances"]}],"questionText":"Question 31 (4 marks)\nA water sample was analysed to determine the chloride ion content. 100.0 mL of this\nwater was added to 25.00 mL of 0.100 mol L−1 AgNO (aq 3 ).\nThe mixture was filtered and the filtrate was titrated against 0.0500 mol L−1 KSCN(aq)\naccording to the following reaction.\nAg+(aq) + SCN−(aq) AgSCN(s)\nThe titration was repeated three times and the average titre was 28.65 mL.\nCalculate the concentration of chloride ions in the water, expressed in mg L−1.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nPlease turn over\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 30 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n5148310372","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q31-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q31-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 31\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly calculates the concentration of chloride ions 4\n• Provides the main steps of the calculation 3\n• Provides some relevant steps of the calculation 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nMole ratio Ag+ : SCN− = 1 : 1\nAmount SCN− used in second titration = cV = 0.0500 mol L−1 × 0.02865 L = 0.0014325 mol\n∴ Amount Ag+ in excess = 0.0014325 mol\nTotal amount Ag+ added at beginning = cV = 0.02500 mol L−1 × 0.100 L = 0.00250 mol\n∴ Amount Ag+ reacting with Cl− = 0.00250 mol − 0.0014325 mol = 0.0010675 mol\n∴ Amount Cl− = 0.0010675 mol\nConcentration Cl− in water: c = n/V = 0.0010675 mol ÷ 0.1000 L = 0.010675 mol L−1\nChloride MM = 35.45 g mol−1\nConcentration Cl− in water = 35.45 g mol−1 × 0.010675 mol L−1\n= 0.378429 g L−1\n= 378 mg L−1","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q32","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":32,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Nomenclature","iq":"How do we systematically name organic chemical compounds?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 7 Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","Mod 7 Nomenclature"]}],"questionText":"Question 32 (4 marks)\nThe table shows three compounds and their boiling points.\nCompound Boiling point\n(°C)\nMethanol 64.7\nPropanoic acid 141.2\nMethyl propanoate 79.8\nAn ester does not always have a lower boiling point than both the alcohol and the\nalkanoic acid from which it is produced.\nUsing the information in the table, account for this observation.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 31 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n3013310371","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q32-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q32-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 32\nCriteria Marks\n• Accounts for the observation using the information provided 4\n• Outlines reasons for the observation using some of the information\nprovided 3\n• Outlines a reason for the observation\nOR 2\n• Identifies reasons for the observation\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nThe boiling points of all three compounds are determined by the intermolecular forces\nbetween their molecules. Both methanol and propanoic acid have hydrogen bonding\nbetween molecules, while the ester (methyl propanoate) does not. As hydrogen bonding is\nthe strongest type of intermolecular force it might be assumed that this would give the ester a\nlower boiling point. However, all three compounds also have dispersion forces between\nmolecules. These increase with the number of electrons per molecule. The dispersion forces\nbetween the ester molecules are larger than the combined effect of dispersion forces and\nhydrogen bonding in the alcohol.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q33","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":33,"section":"II","marks":6,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"},{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)","iq":"How can the position of equilibrium be described and what does the equilibrium constant represent?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-13","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":2,"content":["Mod 6 Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory"]},{"part":"b","marks":4,"content":["Mod 6 Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","Mod 5 Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)"]}],"questionText":"Question 33 (6 marks)\nExcess solid calcium hydroxide is added to a beaker containing 0.100 L of 2.00 mol L−1\nhydrochloric acid and the mixture is allowed to come to equilibrium.\n(a) Show that the amount (in mol) of calcium hydroxide that reacts with the\nhydrochloric acid is 0.100 mol.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\n(b) It is valid in this instance to make the simplifying assumption that the amount\nof calcium ions present at equilibrium is equal to the amount generated in the\nreaction in part (a).\nCalculate the pH of the resulting solution.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 32 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n6604310370","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q33-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q33-mg0.webp","/img/c2020-q33-mg2.webp"],"mgText":"Question 33 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly shows that the amount of calcium hydroxide is 0.100 mol 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nCa(OH)2(s) + 2HCl(aq) → CaCl2(aq) + 2H2O(ℓ)\nn(HCl) = 2.00 mol L−1 × 0.100 L = 0.200 mol\nn(HCl) n(Ca(OH) ) = 2 2\n0.200 mol = 2\n= 0.100 mol\nQuestion 33 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly calculates the pH, showing relevant working 4\n• Provides most of the steps of the calculations 3\n• Provides some steps of the calculations 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\n2+ 0.100 mol ⎡\n⎣Ca ⎤ = = −1 ⎦ 1.00 mol L\n0.100 L\n2\nK = 5.02 × –6 10 = ⎡Ca2+ ⎤ ⎡OH− ⎤ ⎣ ⎦ ⎣ ⎦\nLet ⎡ −1 ⎣OH– ⎤ =⎦ x mol L\n∴ 2 1.00 × x = 5.02 × 10−6\nx = 5.02 × 10−6\n= 2.24 × 10−3 mol L−1\n∴ ⎡OH− ⎤ ⎣ ⎦ = 2.24 × 10−3 mol L−1\npOH = −log10 (2.24 × 10−3 ) = 2.650\npH = 14.00 − 2.650\n= 11.35","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q34","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":34,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Properties of Acids and Bases","iq":"What is an acid and what is a base?"},{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-2","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 6 Properties of Acids and Bases","Mod 6 Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory"]}],"questionText":"Question 34 (4 marks)\nThe effect of concentration on the pH of acrylic acid (C2H3COOH) and hydrochloric\nacid (HCl) solutions is shown in the graph. Both of these acids are monoprotic.\n1×10−6 1×10−1 1×10−2 1×10−3 1×10−4 1×10−5\nAcid concentration (mol L–1)\n0\n1\n2\n3\n4\npH\n5\n6\n7\nHydrochloric acid\nAcrylic acid\nExplain the trends in the graph. Include relevant chemical equations in your answer.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 33 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n9668310373","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q34-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q34-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 34\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nExplains trends in the graph\nIncludes relevant chemical equations\n4\n•\n•\nExplains a trendin the graph and outlines a second trend in the graph\nIncludes at least one relevant chemical equation\n3\n•\nOR\nIdentifies some trends/features from the graph\n2\n• Outlines a trend/feature of the graph\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nThe trend for both acids is for pH to decrease with increasing concentration as [H+\n]\nincreases. The strong acid, HCl, shows a linear trend with pH (the log of [HCl]). This is\nbecause HCl fully dissociates at all concentrations, and therefore the [H+\n] equals [HCl].\nHowever, acrylic acid is a weak acid. The degree of dissociation is dependent on the\nconcentration of the acid. We can see that the trend of the acrylic acid is not linear. It bends\nup at higher concentrations. This reflects the proportionally lower fraction of dissociation.\nThis is given by the following equilibrium expression.\nC H COOH H CO ! – C O + H+\n2 3 2 3\n⎡C H COO– ⎤ ⎡H+ ⎤ ⎢⎣ 2 3 ⎦⎥⎢⎣ ⎥⎦ K = a ⎡C H COOH ⎤ ⎣ 2 3 ⎦\nK ⎡C H COOH ⎤ ⎡ ⎣ H+ ⎤ = a 2 3 ⎦ ⎢⎣ ⎥⎦ ⎡C H COO– ⎤ ⎢⎣ 2 3 ⎥⎦\nAnswers could include:\n• Increasing concentration decreases pH for both acids\n• The pH of the HCl is lower than that of the C H COOH 2 3\n• For C H COOH the degree of ionisation increases as concentration decreases. 2 3","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q35","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":35,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Solution Equilibria","iq":"How does solubility relate to chemical equilibrium?"},{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)","iq":"How can the position of equilibrium be described and what does the equilibrium constant represent?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 5 Solution Equilibria","Mod 5 Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)"]}],"questionText":"Question 35 (4 marks)\nIn aqueous solution, iodide ions ( I− ) react rapidly with iodine ( I2 ) to form triiodide\nions ( I −\n3 ), making the equilibrium system shown in the chemical equation:\nI\n−(aq) + I2(aq) W I −\n3 (aq)\nThe following relationships can be derived from the reaction mechanism:\n[ I\n− ]eq = 2[ I2 ]eq\n[ I\n− ]initial = 4[ I2 ]eq\n+ 3[ I −\n3 ]eq\nwhere ‘initial’ designates the initial concentration and ‘eq’ designates the equilibrium\nconcentration.\nThe absorbance of the solution in the UV−Vis spectrum is given by:\nA = [ I −\n3 ] × 2.76 × 104\nDetermine the value of the equilibrium constant, given that A = 0.745 at equilibrium\nand [ I− ]initial = 7.00 × 10−4 mol L−1.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nPlease turn over\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 34 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n4258310373","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q35-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q35-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 35\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly calculates the equilibrium constant, showing relevant working 4\n• Provides the main steps 3\n• Provides some relevant steps 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nA = [ I − ] × 2.76 × 104\n3\nA 0.745 [ I − ] = = = 2.70 × 10−5 mol L−1\n3 2.76 × 104 2.76 × 104\n[ I\n−] = 4[ I ] + 3[ I − ] initial 2 eq 3 eq\n7.00 × 10−4 mol L−1 = 4[ I\n2]eq\n+ 3 × 2.70 ×10−5 mol L−1\n7.00 × 10−4 mol L−1 − 3 × 2.70 × 10−5 mol L−1\n[ I\n2]eq = 4\n= 1.55 × 10−4 mol L−1\n∴ [ I−] = 2 × 1.55 × 10−4 mol L−1\n= 3.10 × 10−4 mol L−1\n− [ I ] 2.70 × 10−5\n∴ 3 K = = eq [ I−][ I ] 3.10 × 10−4 × 1.55 × 10−4\n2\n= 564","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2020-q36","subject":"Chemistry","year":2020,"questionNumber":36,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Properties of Acids and Bases","iq":"What is an acid and what is a base?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":5,"content":["Mod 6 Properties of Acids and Bases"]}],"questionText":"Question 36 (5 marks)\n100.00 mL of 2.00 mol L−1 HCl(aq) was initially at a temperature of 22.5°C. The\nmass of this solution was 103 g.\n10.0 g of solid NaOH was added to the acid. The specific heat capacity of the resulting\nsolution was 3.99 J g−1 K−1.\nAssuming no energy loss to the environment, calculate the maximum temperature\nreached by the solution.\nUse the following information in your calculations.\nNaOH(s) Na+(aq) + OH−(aq) DH = −44.5 kJ mol−1\nNaOH(aq) + HCl(aq) NaCl(aq) + H2O(l ) DH = −56.1 kJ mol−1\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n5\nEnd of paper\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 35 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2402310372","questionImages":["/img/c2020-q36-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2020-q36-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 36\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly calculates the maximum temperature reached, showing relevant\nworking\n5\n• Provides substantially correct working 4\n• Provides the main steps 3\n• Provides some relevant steps 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\n1. Dissolution of NaOH(s)\nm 10.0\nn = = = NaOH 0.2500 mol\nMM 39.991\nq = –ΔH × n = 5 × 2 44. 0.25 = 11.127 kJ\n2.\t Reaction between NaOH(aq) and HCl(aq)\nNaOH + HCl\nV = 100.00 × 10−3 L\nc = −1\n2.00 mol L\nn = 0.2500 mol\n∴ n = c × V = 0.200 mol\n∴ HCl is limiting reagent\nq = –ΔH × n = 2 56.1 × 0.200 = 11.22 kJ\n3. Total heat supplied = q + 1 q = 2 22.347 kJ\n4. Change in temperature of solution\nq = mcΔT\n22.347 × 3 10 J = (10.0 + 103) g × 3.99 J g–1 K–1 × ΔT\n∴ ΔT = 49.564°C\n∴ T = T + final 49.564°C = initial 72.1°C (to 1 decimal place)","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q1","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":1,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Static and Dynamic Equilibrium","iq":"What happens when chemical reactions do not go through to completion?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"1 Which pair of components must be equal for a chemical system to be at equilibrium?\nA. The rate of the forward reaction and the rate of the reverse reaction\nB. The concentrations of the reactants and the concentrations of the products\nC. The enthalpy of the forward reaction and the enthalpy of the reverse reaction\nD. The time that an atom exists in a reactant molecule and in a product molecule","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q1-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2021-q2","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":2,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-2"],"parts":[],"questionText":"2 Which ion can be detected using a precipitation reaction with silver nitrate?\nA. Ag+\nB. Cl–\nC. Mg2+\nD. NO −\n3","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q2-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2021-q3","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":3,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Nomenclature","iq":"How do we systematically name organic chemical compounds?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-7"],"parts":[],"questionText":"3 The structure of a compound is shown.\nH\nH\nCH\nH O\nH H\nC C\nH\nH\nN C H\nWhat is the preferred IUPAC name of this compound?\nA. N-methylpropanamide\nB. N-methylpropanamine\nC. N-propanylamine\nD. N-propylmethanamide\n– 4 –","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q3-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2021-q4","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":4,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"4 The structure of ethyl pentanoate is shown.\nH\nH\nCH\nH\nH H\nC\nH\nC H\nH\nO C\nO\nH\nH\nC H\nH\nH\nC C\nWhich pair of chemicals would produce ethyl pentanoate by esterification?\nA. Ethene and pentan-1-ol\nB. Ethane and pentanoic acid\nC. Ethanol and pentanoic acid\nD. Ethanoic acid and pentan-1-ol","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q4-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2021-q5","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":5,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"5 A student used the following method to titrate an acetic acid solution of unknown\nconcentration with a standardised solution of dilute sodium hydroxide.\n• Rinse burette with deionised water.\n• Fill burette with sodium hydroxide solution.\n• Rinse pipette and conical flask with acetic acid solution.\n• Pipette 25.00 mL of acetic acid solution into conical flask.\n• Add appropriate indicator to the conical flask.\n• Titrate to endpoint and record volume of sodium hydroxide solution used.\nCompared to the actual concentration of the acetic acid, the calculated concentration\nwill be\nA. lower.\nB. higher.\nC. the same.\nD. different, but higher or lower cannot be predicted.\n– 5 –","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q5-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2021-q6","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":6,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"},{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"6 Which row of the table describes what happens when a solution of a weak acid is diluted?\n(Assume constant temperature.)\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.\nKa Extent of acid ionisation\nDecreases Increases\nDecreases Decreases\nRemains the same Increases\nRemains the same Decreases","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q6-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2021-q7","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":7,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Products of Reactions Involving Hydrocarbons","iq":"What are the products of reactions of hydrocarbons and how do they react?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"7 Methanol undergoes a substitution reaction using hydrogen bromide.\nCompared to methanol, the product of this reaction has a\nA. lower boiling point.\nB. lower molecular mass.\nC. greater solubility in water.\nD. different molecular geometry at the carbon atom.","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q7-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id"