\nCH\nC\nO\nO\nCH\nCH3\nO\nC\nO\nCH\nC\nO\nO\nCH\nCH3\nO\nC\nO\nWhich is the monomer of this polymer?\nA. B.\nC. D.\nO\nOH\nH C 2C CH\nO\nOH\nCH C HO CH2 2\nO\nOH OH\nH C 3C CH\nO\nOH OH\nH C 3C CH CH2","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q10-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2021-q11","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":11,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Factors that Affect Equilibrium","iq":"What factors affect equilibrium and how?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"11 Consider this system in a fixed volume at constant temperature.\nPCl5(s) W PCl3(l) + Cl2(g)\nThis system is initially at equilibrium. A small amount of solid PCl5 is added.\nWhich statement is correct?\nA. The amount of Cl2 will increase.\nB. The amount of PCl3 will decrease.\nC. The amount of Cl2 will not change.\nD. The amount of PCl5 will increase then decrease.\n– 8 –","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q11-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2021-q12","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":12,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"12 The mass spectrum and carbon-13 NMR for an organic compound are shown.\n200 120\nppm\n220 180 160 140 100 80 60 20 40 0\nCarbon-13 NMR\n0\n40\n80\n20\n60\n100\nMass spectrum\nRelative intensity\n100959085807570656055504540353025201510\n120\nm/z\n98\n55\n42\nQuestion 12 continues on page 9\n– 9 –\nQuestion 12 (continued)\nWhich compound could produce the two spectra?\nC. D.\nA. B.\nH\nH\nCH\nH\nH\nC\nO\nC\nH\nH\nC H\nH\nH\nCH\nO\nH\nC\nH\nC\nH\nH H\nC C C\nH\nH\nCC\nCC\nH H\nH\nH\nH\nH\nH\nH\nH\nH\nC\nC\nO\nH H\nH CH3\nC\nC C\nCC\nH H\nH\nO\nH","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q12-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"A"},{"id":"chem-2021-q13","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":13,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Products of Reactions Involving Hydrocarbons","iq":"What are the products of reactions of hydrocarbons and how do they react?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"13 A chemist synthesises a substance using the following pathway.\nhydration oxidation X Y Z\nWhat are compounds X, Y and Z?\nA.\nB.\nC.\nD.\nX Y Z\npropane propan-1-ol propan-2-one\npropane propan-1-ol propanoic acid\nprop-1-ene propan-2-ol propan-2-one\nprop-1-ene propan-2-ol propanoic acid\n– 10 –","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q13-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2021-q14","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":14,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-4"],"parts":[],"questionText":"14 A sample of nickel was dissolved in nitric acid to produce a solution with a volume of\n50.00 mL. 10.00 mL of this solution was then diluted to 250.0 mL. This solution was\nsubjected to colorimetric analysis. A calibration curve for this analysis is given.\n0.0000\n0.00\n0.10\n0.20\n0.30\n0.40\n0.50\n0.60\n0.70\n0.80\n0.0010 0.0020 0.0030\nConcentration (mol L–1)\nAbsorbance\n0.0040 0.0050\nThe solution gave an absorbance value of 0.30.\nWhat was the mass of the sample of nickel?\nA. 0.0021 g\nB. 0.031 g\nC. 0.053 g\nD. 0.15 g","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q14-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2021-q15","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":15,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-4"],"parts":[],"questionText":"15 What is the pH of the resultant solution after 20.0 mL of 0.20 mol L−1\nHCl(aq) is mixed\nwith 20.0 mL of 0.50 mol L−1 NaOH(aq)?\nA. 11.8\nB. 13.2\nC. 13.5\nD. 14.0\n– 11 –","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q15-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2021-q16","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":16,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-5"],"parts":[],"questionText":"16 This titration curve is produced when an acid is titrated with a sodium hydroxide solution\nof the same concentration.\n0.0\n0\n2\n4\n6\n8\n10\n12\n14\n5.0 10.0 15.0\nTitre volume (mL)\npH\n20.0 25.0\nHow many acidic protons does this acid possess?\nA. 1\nB. 2\nC. 3\nD. 4","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q16-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2021-q17","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":17,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"17 A sample was contaminated with sodium phosphate. The sample was dissolved in\nwater and added to an excess of acidified (NH4)\n2MoO4 to produce a precipitate of\n(NH4)\n3PO4•12MoO3 (MM = 1877 g mol–1).\nIf 24.21 g of dry (NH4)\n3PO4•12MoO3 was obtained, what was the mass of sodium\nphosphate in the original sample?\nA. 1.225 g\nB. 1.521 g\nC. 1.818 g\nD. 2.115 g\n– 12 –","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q17-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2021-q18","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":18,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"18 The table lists the information from a proton NMR spectrum.\nChemical shift (ppm) Multiplicity Number of hydrogens\n1.0 Triplet 3\n1.4 Singlet 3\n1.8 Quartet 2\nWhich compound could have produced this spectrum?\nA. 1,2,2-trichlorobutane\nB. 1,3-dichloro-2-methylpropane\nC. 2-chloro-2-methylbutane\nD. 2,2-dichlorobutane","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q18-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2021-q19","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":19,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)","iq":"How can the position of equilibrium be described and what does the equilibrium constant represent?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-4"],"parts":[],"questionText":"19 A quantity of silver nitrate is added to 250.0 mL of 0.100 mol L−1\npotassium sulfate at\n298 K in order to produce a precipitate. Silver nitrate has a molar mass of 169.9 g mol–1.\nWhat mass of silver nitrate will cause precipitation to start?\nA. 0.00510 g\nB. 0.186 g\nC. 0.465 g\nD. 0.854 g","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q19-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2021-q20","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":20,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-6"],"parts":[],"questionText":"20 The trimethylammonium ion, [(CH3)\n3NH]+, is a weak acid. The acid dissociation\nequation is shown.\n[(CH ) + aq 3 3NH] ( ) + H O(l) W H O+\n2 3 (aq) + (CH ) N(aq K –103 3 ) a = 1.55 x 10\nAt 20°C, a saturated solution of trimethylammonium chloride, [(CH3)\n3NH]Cl, has a pH\nof 4.46.\nWhat is the K\nsp of trimethylammonium chloride?\nA. 1.26 × 10–9\nB. 7.76\nC. 60.2\nD. 5.01 × 1010\n© 2021 NSW Education Standards Authority\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 13 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2021 HIGHER SCHOOL CERTIFICATE EXAMINATION\nChemistry\nSection II Answer Booklet\n80 marks\nAttempt Questions 21–36\nAllow about 2 hours and 25 minutes for this section\nInstructions\nCentre Number\nStudent Number\n5638310365\n1011\n• Write your Centre Number and Student Number at the top of this\npage.\n• Answer the questions in the spaces provided. These spaces\nprovide guidance for the expected length of response.\n• Show all relevant working in questions involving calculations.\n15050\nPlease turn over\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 14 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0589310365","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q20-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2021-q21","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":21,"section":"II","marks":6,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Hydrocarbons","iq":"How can hydrocarbons be classified based on their structure and reactivity?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-15","12-2","12-3","12-5"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":2,"content":["Mod 7 Hydrocarbons"]},{"part":"b","marks":2,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Organic Substances"]},{"part":"c","marks":2,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Organic Substances"]}],"questionText":"Question 21 (6 marks)\nFour organic liquids are used in an experiment. The four liquids are\n• hexane\n• hex-1-ene\n• propan-1-ol\n• propanoic acid.\n(a) State ONE safety concern associated with organic liquids and suggest ONE way\nto address this safety concern.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\n(b) The organic liquids are held separately in four flasks but the flasks are not\nlabelled. Tests were conducted to identify these liquids. The outcomes of the\ntests are summarised below.\n2\nFlask Reaction with acidified\noxidant (KMnO4/H+\n)?\nMiscible with\nwater?\n1 No Yes\n2 Yes No\n3 Yes Yes\n4 No No\nIdentify the FOUR liquids.\nFlask Liquid\n1\n2\n3\n4\nQuestion 21 continues on page 15\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 15 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n1024310360\nQuestion 21 (continued)\n(c) What chemical test, other than those used in part (b), could be used to confirm\nthe identification of ONE of the liquids? Include the expected observation in\nyour answer.\n2\nLiquid: ..............................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\nEnd of Question 21\nPlease turn over\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 16 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n5486310366","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q21-0.webp","/img/c2021-q21-1.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q21-mg0.webp","/img/c2021-q21-mg1.webp","/img/c2021-q21-mg3.webp"],"mgText":"Question 21 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nIdentifies a safety concern associated with organic liquids\nStates how the safety concern could be addressed\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nOrganic solvents are generally flammable so no sources of ignition should be present.\nQuestion 21 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly identifies all four liquids 2\n• Correctly identifies one liquid 1\nSample answer:\nFlask Liquid\n1 propanoic acid\n2 hex-1-ene\n3 propan-1-ol\n4 hexane\nQuestion 21 (c)\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nProvides a relevant test for the liquid\nStates the expected observation\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nLiquid: propanoic acid\nAdd a small amount of solid sodium hydrogen carbonate to the organic liquid. Bubbles of gas\nare produced indicating propanoic acid.\nAnswers could include:\nLiquid: propanoic acid\nAdd drops of universal indicator to the organic liquid. If it is propanoic acid, the indicator will\nchange colour from green to yellow/orange/red.\nUse of other indicators such at litmus paper or a pH probe.\nLiquid: hex-1-ene\nAdd drops of bromine water to the organic liquid. If it is hex-1-ene, the bromine water will\ndecolourise.\nLiquid: propan-1-ol\nSodium metal or Lucas test.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q22","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":22,"section":"II","marks":3,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Factors that Affect Equilibrium","iq":"What factors affect equilibrium and how?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":3,"content":["Mod 5 Factors that Affect Equilibrium"]}],"questionText":"Question 22 (3 marks)\nConsider the following equilibrium system.\n2CrO 2−\n4 (aq) + 2H+(aq) W Cr 2−2O7 (aq) + H O(l) DH = –895 kJ mol–1\n2\nyellow orange\n3\nThe solution is orange.\nJustify TWO ways to shift the equilibrium to the left to change the colour of the\nsolution.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q22-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q22-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 22\nCriteria Marks\n• Justifies TWO relevant ways 3\n• Justifies ONE relevant way\nOR 2\n• Identifies TWO relevant ways\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nThe concentration of hydrogen ions could be reduced. This would increase the concentration\nof chromate ions, which will make the solution more yellow.\nHeating the solution will drive the reaction to the left, as the forward reaction is exothermic.\nThus the concentration of yellow chromate ions would increase.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q23","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":23,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"},{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory","iq":"What is the role of water in solutions of acids and bases?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":2,"content":["Mod 7 Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","Mod 6 Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory"]},{"part":"b","marks":2,"content":["Mod 7 Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","Mod 6 Using Brønsted–Lowry Theory"]}],"questionText":"Question 23 (4 marks)\nMethanoic acid reacts with aqueous potassium hydroxide. A salt is produced in this\nreaction.\n(a) Write a balanced chemical equation for this reaction.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\n(b) Is the salt acidic, basic or neutral? Justify your answer.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 17 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n7986310363","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q23-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q23-mg0.webp","/img/c2021-q23-mg1.webp"],"mgText":"Question 23 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Provides a substantially correct balanced equation 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nHCOOH(aq) + KOH(aq) → KHCOO(aq) + H2O(\u0001).\nAnswers could include:\nHCOOH(aq) + KOH(aq) → KHCOO(s) + H2O(\u0001).\nQuestion 23 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nCorrectly identifies salt as basic\nProvides suitable justification\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nPotassium methanoate is a basic salt as HCOO–\nis the conjugate base of a weak acid.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q24","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":24,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Nomenclature","iq":"How do we systematically name organic chemical compounds?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 7 Nomenclature"]}],"questionText":"Question 24 (4 marks)\nA straight-chained alkane has a molar mass of 72.146 g mol–1.\nProvide the structural formulae for this alkane and all other isomers of it in the space\nprovided.\nName these molecules using IUPAC conventions.\n4\nStructural formula Name\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 18 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0736310369","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q24-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q24-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 24\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\n•\nNames pentane as the straight-chained alkane\nProvides the structural formula\nCorrectly provides the structural formulae and names the other two\nisomers\n4\n• Provides THREE correct structural formulae and ONE correct name\nOR\n• Provides TWO correct structural formulae and TWO correct names\n3\n• Provides TWO correct structural formulae without names\nOR\n• Provides TWO correct names without structural formulae\nOR\n• Provides ONE correct structural formula and ONE correct name\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q25","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":25,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 7 Alcohols"]}],"questionText":"Question 25 (4 marks)\nA student conducted an experiment in the school laboratory under standard laboratory\nconditions (25°C, 100 kPa) to determine the volume of carbon dioxide gas produced\nduring the fermentation of glucose. The following apparatus was set up.\n4\nRubber stopper\nGas syringe\nConical flask\nAqueous glucose, yeast and\nNa2HPO4 (yeast nutrient)\nThe following data were collected.\nDay Total volume of gas (mL)\n1 489\n2 677\n3 899\n4 1006\n5 1006\nAssume the total volume of gas produced was due to the production of carbon dioxide.\nCalculate the mass of ethanol produced by the fermentation reaction. Include a\nrelevant chemical equation in your answer.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 19 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n6277310365","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q25-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q25-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 25\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nPerforms correct calculations\nIncludes a relevant chemical equation\n4\n•\n•\nProvides the main steps\nIncludes a chemical equation\n3\n• Provides some relevant steps 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nC6H12O6(aq) → 2C2H5OH(aq) + 2CO2(g)\n1006 mL = 1.006 L CO2\n1.006 L / 24.79 L mol–1 = 0.04058087939 moles of CO2\nnCO2\n= n C2H5OH = 0.04058087939 moles\nmC2 OH = nC2 OH × MMC2 OH = 0.04058087939 mol × 46.068 g mol–1 = 1.869479952 g H5 H5 H5\nOH m = 1.869 g C2H5","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q26","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":26,"section":"II","marks":6,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","iq":"What are the properties of organic acids and bases?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-2","12-6","12-7"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":4,"content":["Mod 7 Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases","Mod 7 Alcohols"]},{"part":"b","marks":2,"content":["Mod 7 Reactions of Organic Acids and Bases"]}],"questionText":"Question 26 (6 marks)\nA sequence of chemical reactions, starting with 2-methylprop-1-ene, is shown in the\nflow chart.\n(a) Complete the flow chart by drawing structural formulae for compounds A, B, C\nand D.\n4\nHydration reaction with dilute H2SO4\nC\nO\nC\nC\nC\nC\nH\nH H\nH\nH H\nH\nH O\nH\nH\nH2C\nC\nCH3\nCH3\nA B\nC D\nSubstitution reaction\nwith concentrated HCl and ZnCl2\nOxidation reaction\nwith acidified KMnO4\nMethyl 2-methylpropanoate\nReflux with methanol and\nconcentrated H2SO4\n(b) Reflux is used in the synthesis of methyl 2-methylpropanoate.\nProvide TWO reasons for using this technique.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 20 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n1032310369","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q26-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q26-mg0.webp","/img/c2021-q26-mg1.webp"],"mgText":"Question 26 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Draws correct structural formulae for compounds A–D 4\n• Draws correct structural formulae for three compounds 3\n• Draws structural formulae demonstrating an understanding of some\ndifferent reactions 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nQuestion 26 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• Gives TWO reasons for refluxing reaction mixture 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nReflux uses heat to speed up the reaction.\nThe condenser retains the volatile reactants and products.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q27","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":27,"section":"II","marks":6,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Solution Equilibria","iq":"How does solubility relate to chemical equilibrium?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-2","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":4,"content":["Mod 5 Solution Equilibria"]},{"part":"b","marks":2,"content":["Mod 5 Solution Equilibria"]}],"questionText":"Question 27 (6 marks)\nAn experiment is carried out to determine the K\nsp value for lithium phosphate (Li3PO4).\nFive samples of Li+ ion solution were prepared, and a different solution of PO 3−\n4 was\nadded to each of them. Columns 2 and 3 of the table show the values before any\nreaction occurs.\nSample [Li+] (mol L−1) 3−\n4 ][PO (mol L−1) Observation\n1 0.15 0.00010 No precipitate\n2 0.15 0.0010 No precipitate\n3 0.15 0.010 No precipitate\n4 0.15 0.10 White precipitate\n5 0.15 1.00 Heavy white precipitate\n(a) Calculate the range within which the K\nsp value of lithium phosphate lies.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n4\n(b) Justify ONE way in which the procedure of this investigation could be improved\nto increase the accuracy of the calculated result.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 21 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0136310363","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q27-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q27-mg0.webp","/img/c2021-q27-mg1.webp"],"mgText":"Question 27 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly calculates the range with the correct significant figures 4\n• Provides some relevant steps 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\n3\nK ⎡ ⎤ ⎡ 3− ⎤ = Li+ PO\nsp ⎣⎢ ⎦⎥ ⎣⎢ 4 ⎦⎥\nSample 3\n3\nK ⎡ ⎤ ⎡ 3− ⎤ = Li+ PO\nsp ⎣⎢ ⎦⎥ ⎣⎢ 4 ⎦⎥\n= (0.15)\n3\n× (0.010) = 0.00003375\n= 0.000034 to 2 significant figures\nSample 4\n3\nK ⎡ ⎤ ⎡ 3− ⎤ = Li+ PO\nsp ⎣⎢ ⎦⎥ ⎣⎢ 4 ⎦⎥\n= (0.15)\n3\n× (0.10) = 0.0003375\n= 0.00034 to 2 significant figures\nPrecipitation will occur somewhere between sample 3 and sample 4. The Ksp value is\nbetween 3.4 × 10–5 and 3.4 × 10–4.\nQuestion 27 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• States one way to improve the investigation and relates that to the\naccuracy of the calculated result 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nIncrease the number of solutions of PO4\n3− ions in the concentration range of 0.010 mol L−1\nto 0.10 mol L−1. This will narrow the range of values and bring the estimated value closer to\nthe SI value of lithium phosphate.\nAnswers could include:\nTitrate the Li+ ions against a PO4\n3– ion solution until a permanent precipitate is formed. This\nwill give an experimental value that is correct or close to correct to the SI value for lithium\nphosphate.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q28","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":28,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Inorganic Substances"]}],"questionText":"Question 28 (4 marks)\nA 5.30 g sample of an alkali metal hydroxide was dissolved in water. After mixing\nwith excess Cu(NO3)\n2, the precipitate was collected, dried, measured and found to\nhave a mass of 4.61 g.\nIdentify the alkali metal hydroxide. Support your answer with calculations and a\nbalanced equation.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 22 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n7037310360","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q28-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q28-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 28\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nCorrectly identifies the alkali hydroxide\nProvides correct calculations and a balanced equation\n4\n• Provides the main steps of the calculation 3\n• Provides some relevant steps 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nAlkali metals = group 1\nCu NO ( (aq) + 2XOH(aq) → Cu OH (s) + 2XNO 3 ) ( )2 3(aq) 2\nNet Ionic equation: Cu2+ (aq) + 2OH− (aq) → Cu OH ( ) (s) 2\nMass Cu OH)2 ( (s) = 4.61 g\nMoles Cu OH ( ) (s) 4.61 g ÷ MM Cu OH 2 = ( ( )2 ) = 4.61 g ÷ 97.566 g mol–1\n= 0.04725 mol\nn = 2n = 0.09450 mol OH–\nCu OH ( )2 OH−\n2n = n = 0.09450 mol\nOH− XOH−\namount = mass ÷ molar mass\n∴ molar mass = mass ÷ amount\nMM = 5.30 g ÷ 0.09450 mol = 56.085 g mol–1\nMass of OH = (16 + 1.008) = 17.008 g mol–1\nMass of Alkali Metal = 56.085 g mol–1 – 17.008 g mol–1 = 39.077 g mol–1\n39.077 g mol–1 ≅ 39.10 g mol–1 (Molar Mass of K from Periodic table)\nTherefore, the unknown alkali hydroxide = Potassium Hydroxide.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q29","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":29,"section":"II","marks":7,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Organic Substances","iq":"How is information about the reactivity and structure of organic compounds obtained?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":7,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Organic Substances"]}],"questionText":"Question 29 (7 marks)\nA chemist obtained spectral data of pentane-1,5-diamine (C5H14N2).\nH2N NH2\n7\n4000 3000 2000\nWavenumber (cm−1)\n1600 1200 800 0\n50\n100\n% transmittance\nInfrared spectrum\n10 20 30 40 50 60\nm/z\nMass spectrum\n70 80 90 100\n0\n40\n80\n60\n20\n100\n120\nRelative intensity\n(Mass of molecular ion: 102)\n200 180 160 140 120 100 80 60 40 20 0\nCarbon-13 NMR\nChemical shift (ppm)\nQuestion 29 continues on page 23\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 23 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2207310364\nQuestion 29 (continued)\n3.5 3.0 2.5 2.0 1.5 1.0 0.5 0.0\nChemical shift (ppm)\nProton NMR\n4 × H\ntriplet 4 × H\nquintet\n6 × H\noverlapping quintets\nRelate the highlighted features of the spectra to the structure of pentane-1,5-diamine.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\nQuestion 29 continues on page 24\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 24 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n1619310360\nQuestion 29 (continued)\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\nEnd of Question 29\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 25 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0585310361","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q29-0.webp","/img/c2021-q29-1.webp","/img/c2021-q29-2.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q29-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 29\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nDemonstrates a comprehensive understanding of the relationship\nbetween the highlighted features of the spectra and the structure of\npentane-1,5-diamine\nRefers to the relevant spectroscopic data in each of the four spectra\n7\n• Demonstrates a thorough understanding of the relationship between the\nhighlighted features of the spectra and the structure of pentane-1,5-\ndiamine 6\n• Refers to relevant spectroscopic data in at least three of the spectra\n• Demonstrates a sound understanding of the relationship between the\nhighlighted features of the spectra and the structure of pentane-1,5-\ndiamine 4–5\n• Refers to relevant spectroscopic data in at least two of the spectra\n• Demonstrates some understanding of the interpretation of spectroscopic\ndata 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nAnswers could include:\nInfrared spectrum\n•\t Peak at wave number range 3300–3400 cm–1 is due to N-H group (amino group)\nThe spectrum confirms the presence of amino group.\nMass spectrum\n•\t The highlighted feature is the fragment CH2NH2\n+ (12 × 1 + 1.0 × 4 + 14 × 1 = 30)\nCarbon-13 NMR spectrum\n•\t There are 5 carbon atoms in the molecule, however, there are only three peaks (three\nsignals) shown in the spectrum.\n•\t Due to symmetry, carbon atoms 1 and 5 are in identical environments. The same is true\nfor carbon atoms 2 and 4. Carbon atom 3 is in a unique environment.\n•\t The signals at 24 and 33 ppm are consistent with –CH2–CH2– carbon atoms (5−40 ppm)\n•\t The signal at 42 ppm is due to the C-N-H groups (25−60 ppm)\nProton NMR\n•\t Quintets arise from H atoms with four H atoms on neighbouring C atoms, e.g. the H atoms\non C-3 have four neighbouring H atoms on C-2 and C-4.\n•\t The highlighted signal results from similar chemical shifts of protons in two different\nenvironments.\n•\t The highlighted signal results from overlap of a 2H signal and a 4H signal, giving 6 H.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q30","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":30,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-2","12-4"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":5,"content":["Mod 8 Analysis of Inorganic Substances"]}],"questionText":"Question 30 (5 marks)\nA student was trying to identify the ions present in a dilute aqueous solution.\nThe solution contained ions of barium, calcium or magnesium, and ions of hydroxide\nor acetate.\nThe student performed the following tests and recorded their observations. A fresh\nsample of the solution was used for each test.\n• When aqueous sodium chloride was added, no visible reaction was observed.\n• When aqueous silver nitrate was added, brown precipitate was produced. The\nprecipitate dissolved when dilute hydrochloric acid was added.\n• When concentrated aqueous sodium sulfate was added, white precipitate was\nproduced.\nEvaluate this procedure as a method of identifying the ions.\n5\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 26 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n6577310368","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q30-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q30-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 30\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\n•\nShows a comprehensive understanding of the procedure\nOutlines positive and negative aspects of the procedure\nMakes an informed judgement\n5\n•\n•\nOutlines some positive and negative aspects of the procedure\nMakes a judgement\n4\n• Outlines some positive and/or negative aspects of the procedure 3\n•\nOR\nIdentifies positive and/or negative aspects of the procedure\n2\n• Outlines a positive OR negative aspect of the procedure\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nThe first test is not necessary to identify either the anion or cation. Sodium won’t precipitate\nwith either of the anions and chloride will not precipitate with any of the possible cations.\nAdding silver ions to the solution in the second test is appropriate to identify the anion. Silver\nacetate is a soluble salt, but silver hydroxide is an insoluble, brown salt which dissolves when\nHCl is added. Therefore the anion must be hydroxide.\nThe second test also eliminates magnesium as the cation, as magnesium hydroxide is\ninsoluble.\nA third test is needed to distinguish between barium and calcium. However, both barium and\ncalcium will produce a precipitate with concentrated sulfate ions as seen in the observations,\nso this test can’t accurately distinguish between barium and calcium ions.\nThis procedure is insufficient to completely identify both ions in the compound and so is not\nan appropriate method to identify the unknown compound. A flame test is needed to identify\nthe cation.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q31","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":31,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)","iq":"How can the position of equilibrium be described and what does the equilibrium constant represent?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 5 Calculating the Equilibrium Constant (Keq)"]}],"questionText":"Question 31 (4 marks)\nAmmonia is produced according to the following equilibrium equation.\nN2(g) + 3H2(g) W 2NH (g 3 )\nThere are 4.50 moles of nitrogen gas, 1.00 mole of hydrogen gas and 5.80 moles of\nammonia in a 10.0 L vessel. The system is at equilibrium at 298 K. The value of K\neq\nat this temperature is 748.\nHow many moles of nitrogen gas need to be added to the vessel to increase the amount\nof ammonia by 0.050 moles?\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 27 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n4074310368","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q31-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q31-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 31\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly calculates the moles of nitrogen 4\n• Provides the main steps of the calculation 3\n• Provides some relevant steps of the calculation 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nN2 + 3H2 \u0001 2NH3\n4.5 moles + 1.0 moles 5.8 moles\nx moles\n− 0.025 moles − 0.075 moles + 0.05 moles\n4.475 + x moles 0.925 moles 5.85 moles\n4.475 + x mol L–1 0.0925 mol L–1 0.585 mol L–1\n10\n2\n⎡\n⎣\n⎡\n⎣\n⎡\n⎣\nNH3\nN H\n⎤\n⎦ K =\neq 3 ⎤\n⎦ ⎤ 2 2 ⎦\n0.5852\n748 = 4.475 + x\n× 0.09253\n10\n⎛ 4.475 + x\n× 0.09253\n10\n⎞\n⎠\n⎟\n= 0.5852 748 ×\n⎝\n⎜\n4.475 + x 0.5852\n× 0.09253 = 10 748\n4.475 + x 0.5852\n= 10 748 × 0.09253\n10 × 0.5852\n4.475 + x =\n748 × 0.09253\n10 × 0.5852\nx = − 4.475\n748 × 0.09253\nx = 1.3 moles\n1.3 moles of nitrogen must be added to the equilibrium mixture.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q32","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":32,"section":"II","marks":4,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Properties of Acids and Bases","iq":"What is an acid and what is a base?"},{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":4,"content":["Mod 6 Properties of Acids and Bases","Mod 6 Quantitative Analysis"]}],"questionText":"Question 32 (4 marks)\nThe molar enthalpies of neutralisation of three reactions are given.\nReaction 1:\nHCl(aq) + KOH(aq) KCl(aq) + H2O(l) DH = –57.6 kJ mol−1\nReaction 2:\nHNO (aq) + KOH(aq 3(aq 3 ) KNO ) + H2O(l) DH = –57.6 kJ mol−1\nReaction 3:\nHCN(aq) + KOH(aq) KCN(aq) + H2O(l) DH = –12.0 kJ mol−1\nExplain why the first two reactions have the same enthalpy value but the third reaction\nhas a different value.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 28 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n9566310369","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q32-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q32-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 32\nCriteria Marks\n• Shows a thorough understanding of why the first two values are the same\nand why the last value is different 4\n• Shows a sound understanding of why the first two values are the same\nand why the last value is different 3\n• Shows some understanding of the reactions 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nThe net ionic equation for all three reactions is H+(aq) + OH–\n(aq) → H2O(\u0001).\nHCl and HNO3 are strong acids and completely ionised. When they react with aqueous KOH\nthey both have the same exothermic enthalpy value due to the same net ionic equation.\nWhen weak acid HCN reacts with aqueous KOH, HCN starts off only partially ionised in an\nequilibrium reaction with water HCN(aq) + H2O(\u0001)\u0002 H3O+(aq) + CN–\n(aq). As the reaction\nproceeds, the HCN will further ionise as the equilibrium shifts to the right. This is endothermic\nso it removes some heat from the system and the overall reaction is less exothermic than the\nfirst two reactions. The reaction has a smaller but still exothermic ΔH \u0001 value.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q33","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":33,"section":"II","marks":6,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Static and Dynamic Equilibrium","iq":"What happens when chemical reactions do not go through to completion?"}],"outcomes":["12-12","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"a","marks":2,"content":["Mod 5 Static and Dynamic Equilibrium"]},{"part":"b","marks":4,"content":["Mod 5 Static and Dynamic Equilibrium"]}],"questionText":"Question 33 (6 marks)\nThe relationships between DH and TDS with temperature for a chemical system are\ndisplayed in the graph.\n200 220 240 260 280 320\nTemperature (K)\n300 340 360 380 400\n–140\n–100\n–40\n–80\n–120\n–60\n–20\nT1 T2 T3\nDH, TD\nS (kJ mol–1)\n)H\nT)S\n(a) Calculate DG for this system at 300 K.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n2\n(b) What can be deduced about the system when the temperature is T1, T2 and T3?\nSupport your answer with reference to the graph.\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n...............................................................................................................................\n4\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 29 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n0008310361","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q33-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q33-mg0.webp","/img/c2021-q33-mg2.webp"],"mgText":"Question 33 (a)\nCriteria Marks\n• Correctly calculates ΔG 2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nFrom graph, TΔS = –78 kJ/mol, ΔH = –93 kJ/mol\nΔG = ΔH – TΔS = –93 kJ/mol – –78 kJ/mol = –15 kJ/mol\nAnswers could include:\nAcceptable range of ΔH and TΔS to be determined at Marking Centre.\nQuestion 33 (b)\nCriteria Marks\n• Makes correct deductions about the system at temperatures T1, T2 and\nT3 4\n• Supports answer with reference to the graph\n•\n•\nMakes correct deductions about the system at two of the temperatures\nRefers to the graph\n3\n• Makes a relevant deduction about the system at temperature(s) T1 and/or\nT2 and/or T3\n2\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nAt all three temperatures the reaction is exothermic, as ΔH is negative. The entropy of\nreaction, ΔS is also negative as TΔS is negative, and T is always positive. From the\nrelationship ΔG = ΔH − TΔS we can see that at T1, ΔG is negative and therefore the reaction\nis spontaneous. At T2 ΔG = 0 and therefore the system is in equilibrium. At T3, ΔG is positive\nand therefore the reaction is non-spontaneous.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q34","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":34,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-4","12-5","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":5,"content":["Mod 6 Quantitative Analysis"]}],"questionText":"Question 34 (5 marks)\nGaseous HCl was bubbled into water and two solutions, X and Y. Solutions X and Y\ncontain the same type of ions. The pH of each was monitored over time and recorded\nin the graph shown.\n5\n7\npH\n6\n5\n4\n3\n2\n1\n0\nt\n0 t1 t2\nTime\nKEY\nWater\nX\nY\nExplain the observed pH of the water and each of the solutions at t\n0, t1 and t2. Include\na relevant balanced chemical equation in your answer.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 30 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n8952310367","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q34-0.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q34-mg0.webp"],"mgText":"Question 34\nCriteria Marks\n• Explains the pH of water, solution X and solution Y\n• Includes a relevant balanced equation\n5\n• Shows a sound understanding of the pH of the water, solution X and\nsolution Y\n• Includes a balanced equation\n4\n• Shows some understanding of the pH of the water and/or solution X\nand/or solution Y\nAND/OR\n• Includes a balanced equation\n2–3\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nAt t\n0 the pH of water is 7. X and Y have pH 4.9 so are acidic.\nAt t\n1, the pH of water is dropping rapidly due to production of H3O+. The pH of X and Y have\nonly dropped slightly, therefore they are buffers. When HCl is added it donates a proton to\nthe base, producing HA instead of H3O+ minimising the change in pH:\nHCl(g) + A–(aq) \u0002\u0001HA(aq) + Cl– (aq)\nAt t\n2, the pH of X and Y have begun to decrease, but the pH of X is lower. A–\nhas been used\nup so [H3O+] increases lowering the pH. The pH is lower for X because it was a less\nconcentrated buffer initially and the A–\nwas used up more rapidly.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q35","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":35,"section":"II","marks":7,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"},{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Alcohols","iq":"How can alcohols be produced and what are their properties?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-14","12-4","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":7,"content":["Mod 6 Quantitative Analysis","Mod 7 Alcohols"]}],"questionText":"Question 35 (7 marks)\nA manufacturer requires that its product contains at least 85% v/v ethanol. 7\nThe concentration of ethanol in water can be determined by a back titration. Ethanol\nis first oxidised to ethanoic acid using an excess of acidified potassium dichromate\nsolution.\n3C aq 2− aq + aq aq 3+ aq l 2H5OH( ) + 2Cr2O7 ( ) + 16H ( ) 3CH3COOH( ) + 4Cr ( ) + 11H2O( )\nThe remaining dichromate ions are reacted with excess iodide ions to produce\niodine (I2).\nCr O 2−(aq) + 14H+ aq) + 6I− 7 (aq 2 ( ) 2Cr3+(aq) + 7H O(l) + 3I (aq 2 2 )\nThe iodine produced is then titrated with sodium thiosulfate (Na2S2O3).\nI (aq 2 ) + 2S O 2−\n3 (aq) 2I−(aq) + S4O 2−(aq 2 6 )\nA 25.0 mL sample of the manufacturer’s product was diluted with distilled water\nto 1.00 L. A 25.0 mL aliquot of the diluted solution was added to 20.0 mL of\n0.500 mol L−1 acidified potassium dichromate solution in a conical flask. Potassium\niodide (2.0 g) was added and the solution titrated with 0.900 mol L−1 * sodium\nthiosulfate. This was repeated three times.\n*The quantity of potassium iodide should be 5.0 g. This is not required for the calculation.\nThe following results were obtained.\nTime Volume of Na2S2O3(aq) added (mL)\n1 29.9\n2 28.7\n3 28.4\n4 28.6\nThe density of ethanol is 0.789 g mL−1.\nQuestion 35 continues on page 31\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 31 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2306310364\nQuestion 35 (continued)\nDoes the sample meet the manufacturer’s requirements? Support your answer with\ncalculations.\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\n.........................................................................................................................................\nEnd of Question 35\nPlease turn over\nOffice Use Only – Do NOT write anything, or make any marks below this line.\n– 32 –\nDo NOT write in this area.\n2770310369","questionImages":["/img/c2021-q35-0.webp","/img/c2021-q35-1.webp"],"mgImages":["/img/c2021-q35-mg0.webp","/img/c2021-q35-mg1.webp"],"mgText":"Question 35\nCriteria Marks\n•\n•\nCorrectly calculates the concentration of ethanol in the undiluted sample\nin % v/v\nRemoves the outlier from the calculation of thiosulfate volume 7\n• Provides a judgement about the ethanol concentration consistent with the\ncalculations\n•\n•\nProvides substantially correct steps for calculating the concentration of\nethanol\nProvides a judgement about the ethanol concentration consistent with the\ncalculations\n6\n• Provides the main steps for calculating the concentration of ethanol 4–5\n• Provides some steps for calculating the concentration of ethanol 2–3\n• Provides some relevant information 1\nSample answer:\nTitration 1 is an outlier and excluded from the average.\n2− 28.7 + 28.4 + 28.6 Average V S O = = 28.5667 mL = 0.0285667 L ( 2 3 ) 3\nn S O 2− = cV = 0.900 mol L−1 × 0.0285667 L = 0.02571 mol ( 2 3 )\nn S O = n I = 2:1 ∴ n I = × 0.02571 = 0.012853 mol ( 2−\n) ( ) ( ) 2\n1\n2 3 2 2\n( ) O 2− n I : n excess Cr = 3:1 2 ( 2 7 )\n2− ∴ n excess Cr O = × 0.012853 = 0.004285 mol ( 2 7 ) 3\n1\n2− n(initial Cr O ) = cV = 0.500 mol L–1 × 0.0200 L = 0.0100 mol 2 7\n2− 2− n Cr O 2− ( reacted with ethanol = n initial Cr O − n excess Cr O 2 7 ) ( 2 7 ) ( 2 7 ) = 0.0100 − 0.004285 = 0.005715 mol\nn(Cr2O7\n2− reacted with ethanol ) : n(ethanol) = 2:3\n3\n∴ n(ethanol) = × 0.005715 = 0.0085725 mol\n2\nm(C H OH) = n × MM = 0.0085725 mol × (2 × 12.01 + 6 × 1.008 + 16.00) g mol−1\n2 5\n= 0.00857 mol × 46.068 g mol L–1 = 0.394918 g in 25.0 mL diluted solution\n1000.0\nm (C H OH = × 0.394918\u0001 = 15.797\u0001 g in 25.0 mL undiluted sample 2 5 ) 25.0\nV (C H OH) = 15.797\u0001 g ÷ 0.789 g mL−1 = 20.02\u0001 mL 2 5\n% C2H5 ( ) OH = (20.02 mL ÷ 25.0 mL) × 100 = 80.08%\u0001 = 80% v/v\nAs the concentration found is under 85%, the product does not meet the manufacturer’s\nrequirement.","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2021-q36","subject":"Chemistry","year":2021,"questionNumber":36,"section":"II","marks":5,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-4","12-6"],"parts":[{"part":"","marks":5,"content":["Mod 6 Quantitative Analysis"]}],"questionText":"Question 36 (5 marks)\nThe pKa of sulfurous acid in the following reaction is 1.82.\nH SO (aq) + H O(l) W H O+(aq 2 3 2 3 ) + HSO 