3O+(aq) + HSO3\n−(aq) K (1) = 1.51 × 10−2\na\n(2) HSO3\n−(aq) + H2O(\u0001) \u0002 H3O+(aq) + SO32−(\u0001) K (2) = 6.76 × 10−8\na\n⎡ H O+ ⎤ ⎡ HSO − ⎤ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ K (1) = a ⎡ ⎤ H SO ⎣⎢ 2 3 ⎦⎥\n⎡ H O+ ⎤ ⎡ SO 2− ⎤ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ K (2) = a ⎡ HSO − ⎤ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥\nFor H2SO3(aq) + 2H2O(\u0001) \u0002 2H3O+(aq) + SO3\n2−(aq)\n⎡ O+ ⎤\n2\nH ⎡ SO 2− ⎤ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ K = eq ⎡ ⎤ H SO ⎣⎢ 2 3 ⎦⎥\n⎡ O+ ⎤\n2\nH ⎡ HSO − ⎤ ⎡ SO 2− ⎤ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ K (1) × K (2) = a a ⎡ HSO − ⎤ ⎡ H SO ⎤ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥⎣⎢ 2 3 ⎦⎥\n⎡ H O+ ⎤\n2 ⎡ HSO – ⎤ ⎡ SO 2− ⎤ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ = ⎡ HSO − ⎤ ⎡ H SO ⎤ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ ⎣⎢ 2 3 ⎦⎥\n⎡ H O+ ⎤\n2 ⎡ SO 2− ⎤ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ ⎣⎢ 3 ⎦⎥ = ⎡ ⎤ H SO ⎣⎢ 2 3 ⎦⎥\n= K\neq\nKeq = 1.51 × 10−2 × 6.76 × 10−8\n= 1.0 × 10−9","answer":null},{"id":"chem-2022-q1","subject":"Chemistry","year":2022,"questionNumber":1,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 7: Organic Chemistry","topic":"Polymers","iq":"What are the properties and uses of polymers?"}],"outcomes":["12-14","12-7"],"parts":[],"questionText":"1 What term is used to define the repeating unit of a polymer?\nA. Dimer\nB. Isomer\nC. Monomer\nD. Primer","questionImages":["/img/c2022-q1-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2022-q2","subject":"Chemistry","year":2022,"questionNumber":2,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 6: Acid/Base Reactions","topic":"Quantitative Analysis","iq":"How are solutions of acids and bases analysed?"}],"outcomes":["12-13","12-3"],"parts":[],"questionText":"2 When a solution of a primary standard is prepared for titration, which of the following is\nrequired?\nA. A burette\nB. A balance\nC. An indicator\nD. A condenser","questionImages":["/img/c2022-q2-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2022-q3","subject":"Chemistry","year":2022,"questionNumber":3,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 5: Equilibrium and Acid Reactions","topic":"Static and Dynamic Equilibrium","iq":"What happens when chemical reactions do not go through to completion?"}],"outcomes":["12-1","12-12"],"parts":[],"questionText":"3 Which of the following features is NOT a characteristic of a state of equilibrium?\nA. Equilibrium is achieved in a closed system.\nB. Equilibrium position depends on temperature.\nC. Equilibrium can be reached from either direction.\nD. Equilibrium concentrations of reactants and products are equal.\n– 3 –","questionImages":["/img/c2022-q3-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"D"},{"id":"chem-2022-q4","subject":"Chemistry","year":2022,"questionNumber":4,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-4"],"parts":[],"questionText":"4 An analytical chemist was using atomic absorption spectroscopy (AAS) to determine the\nmanganese concentration in a sample.\nThe following diagram shows the absorbance lines of manganese.\nWavelength (nm)\nManganese\n380 750\nThe diagrams below show the emission spectra of four AAS lamps.\nWhich lamp should be used to determine the manganese concentration in the sample?\nWavelength (nm)\nA.\n380 750\nWavelength (nm)\nB.\n380 750\nWavelength (nm)\nC.\n380 750\nD.\n380 Wavelength (nm) 750\n– 4 –","questionImages":["/img/c2022-q4-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"C"},{"id":"chem-2022-q5","subject":"Chemistry","year":2022,"questionNumber":5,"section":"I","marks":1,"tags":[{"module":"Module 8: Applying Chemical Ideas","topic":"Analysis of Inorganic Substances","iq":"How are the ions present in the environment identified and measured?"}],"outcomes":["12-15","12-3"],"parts":[],"questionText":"5 Which pair of ions can be distinguished using a flame test in the school laboratory?\nA. Ag+ and Mg2+\nB. Ba2+ and Ca2+\nC. Br− and Cl−\nD. Fe2+ and Fe3+","questionImages":["/img/c2022-q5-0.webp"],"mgImages":[],"mgText":"","answer":"B"},{"id":"chem-2022-q6","subject":"Chemistry","year":2022,"questio